CREATE TEMP TABLE _s495_adam_eve_c (src_edition TEXT, src_slug TEXT, src_ch INT, src_v INT, src_text TEXT, tgt_edition TEXT, tgt_slug TEXT, tgt_ch INT, tgt_v INT, tgt_text TEXT, tier TEXT, note TEXT) ON COMMIT DROP;
CREATE TEMP TABLE _s495_adam_eve_t (slug TEXT, title TEXT, summary_md TEXT, ch INT, a_vn INT, z_vn INT, tier TEXT, sort_order INT) ON COMMIT DROP;

It ain't new that the tree of life is held back, and it ain't new who is let in again. The last page of the canon says it plainly: *Blessed are they that do his commandments, that they may have right to the tree of life, and may enter in through the gates into the city.* (Revelation 22:14) The way back to the tree is the way of the commandments, and the gates are opened to those who keep them. The canon sets the tree at the end and the commandments on the road to it.$n$,6,1,1,'extras',94000),
('adam-eve-11-adam-reproves-eve-for-the-tree',$n$Adam reproves Eve — and the blame that passes across the household$n$,$n$After the beasts turn on them, Adam turns on Eve: *How was thy mouth opened to eat of the tree concerning which Elohim (God) enjoined thee not to eat of it* (Adam and Eve 11:1). The question is fair, and Moses shows how old the habit of the answer is. When Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) asked, the man said: *And the man said, The woman whom thou gavest to be with me, she gave me of the tree, and I did eat.* (Genesis 3:12) The finger went to the woman, and through her to the One who gave her.

But the word of Yahuah (the LORD) did not stop at the woman. *And unto Adam he said, Because thou hast hearkened unto the voice of thy wife, and hast eaten of the tree, of which I commanded thee, saying, Thou shalt not eat of it: cursed is the ground for thy sake; in sorrow shalt thou eat of it all the days of thy life;* (Genesis 3:17) The man heard, and the man ate, and the sentence on the ground is spoken to him. Eve's mouth was opened, and so was Adam's ear, and neither can be set down as the only cause. Genesis does not hide the woman's part, and it does not let the man stand free of his. This book keeps the argument between husband and wife going, and the canon lets both stand answerable and leaves the way open for both to turn.$n$,11,1,1,'extras',94005),
('adam-eve-15-guarding-paradise-in-the-allotted-portion',$n$Guarding paradise, each in the allotted portion$n$,$n$Eve begins the telling of how the enemy deceived them: *we were guarding paradise, each of us the portion allotted to us from Elohim (God)* (Adam and Eve 15:1). The task is old. In Genesis the man was not set in the garden to rest only, but to work it: *And Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) took the man, and put him into the garden of Eden to dress it and to keep it.* (Genesis 2:15) Dressing and keeping are two halves of one charge, and keeping means watching over.

Eve goes on to say that the creatures were divided between them, the males to Adam and the females to her. Moses does not say that, and the witness itself puts the line in brackets. What Genesis does show is the creatures coming to Adam: *And out of the ground Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) formed every beast of the field, and every fowl of the air; and brought them unto Adam to see what he would call them: and whatsoever Adam called every living creature, that was the name thereof.* (Genesis 2:19) The dominion over the beasts begins with him, and with that the question of who kept what, and where the keeper was not looking, is already waiting. The scene Eve describes is the watch before the failure of the watch.$n$,15,1,1,'extras',94010),
('adam-eve-16-the-devil-counsels-the-serpent',$n$The devil counsels the serpent$n$,$n$Eve's account opens the door behind the garden scene: *Fear not, only be my vessel and I will speak through thy mouth words to deceive him* (Adam and Eve 16:2). Moses gives us only what was heard, and it is exactly what the vessel would say: *Now the serpent was more subtil than any beast of the field which Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) had made. And he said unto the woman, Yea, hath Elohim (God) said, Ye shall not eat of every tree of the garden?* (Genesis 3:1) A question was put in the mouth of the serpent, and the question has a speaker.

John gives the speaker his names. *And the great dragon was cast out, that old serpent, called the Devil, and Satan, which deceiveth the whole world: he was cast out into the earth, and his angels were cast out with him.* (Revelation 12:9) The old serpent, the Devil, and Satan are one, and he is a deceiver of the whole world. The serpent's fear of Yahuah (the LORD) in this book is one of the places where the retelling adds colour; what Genesis and Revelation hold is steady. The first deception began with a question about what Elohim (God) had said, and it was a spoken thing by one who wanted the man out of the garden. It ain't new that the voice behind the serpent has a name, and that the canon knew him from the first page to the last.$n$,16,1,2,'extras',94015),
('adam-eve-27-thou-art-just-and-thy-judgement-is-righteous',$n$Thou art just — the driving out and the plea$n$,$n$They are driven out amid lamentation, and Adam asks for a little space to entreat Yahuah (the Lord). Then Yahuah (the Lord) speaks to the angels, and they fall down and answer: *Thou art just, O Yahuah (Lord), and thou judgest righteous judgement* (Adam and Eve 27:1). Moses tells the driving out without a word of pleading: *So he drove out the man; and he placed at the east of the garden of Eden Cherubims, and a flaming sword which turned every way, to keep the way of the tree of life.* (Genesis 3:24) The cherubim and the sword are the answer the garden gives to the man who ate.

The psalmist says what the angels say, and says it out of his own trouble: *Righteous art thou, O Yahuah (LORD), and upright are thy judgments.* (Psalm 119:137) That is how the canon holds both together, a sentence that is right and an Elohim (God) who hears the one who pleads. Adam does not argue the judgment, he only asks to entreat; and the angels do not argue it either. It ain't new that the judgement is just. What this book adds is the quiet moment in which the first man is heard and not excused.$n$,27,1,1,'extras',94020),
('adam-eve-30-guard-yourselves-from-transgressing-against-the-good',$n$Guard yourselves — the telling handed down$n$,$n$Eve closes the long account of the deception with a word to her descendants: *do ye guard yourselves from transgressing against the good* (Adam and Eve 30:1). The account is for them. In Genesis her own telling is a single line: *And Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) said unto the woman, What is this that thou hast done? And the woman said, The serpent beguiled me, and I did eat.* (Genesis 3:13) This book lengthens it, and what it is for is made plain by the words that follow.

Moses gives the pattern to Yashar'el (Israel) in the same shape: *Only take heed to thyself, and keep thy soul diligently, lest thou forget the things which thine eyes have seen, and lest they depart from thy heart all the days of thy life: but teach them thy sons, and thy sons’ sons;* (Deuteronomy 4:9) The fathers who saw something are charged to hand it down to sons and sons' sons, so that it will not depart from the heart. It ain't new that a failure is to be told, not buried. The household that passes on how it was deceived is doing what the Torah asks of Yashar'el (Israel), keeping a testimony alive for the next generation, and that is the reason the story is told.$n$,30,1,1,'extras',94025),
('adam-eve-38-the-lord-of-hosts-comes-to-the-body-of-adam',$n$The Lord of Hosts comes down to the body of Adam$n$,$n$The archangel asks about the laying out of the remains, and Elohim (God) commands the angels to assemble. Then comes the descent: *the 'Lord of Hosts' came on and four winds drew Him and cherubim mounted on the winds* (Adam and Eve 38:1). The picture is older than this book: *And he rode upon a cherub, and did fly: yea, he did fly upon the wings of the wind.* (Psalm 18:10) The coming of Yahuah (the LORD) on the wings of the wind, with a cherub beneath, is David's song before it is anyone's vision of an angelic funeral.

One man stays awake when the fragrance falls on all the sons of Adam: *Seth alone, because he was born 'according to the appointment of Elohim (God) '* (Adam and Eve 38:1). Moses has the word from the mother's mouth: *And Adam knew his wife again; and she bare a son, and called his name Seth: For Elohim (God), said she, hath appointed me another seed instead of Abel, whom Cain slew.* (Genesis 4:25) It ain't new that the line runs through the appointed one. The canon names Seth as the seed set in the place of Abel, and this book keeps that memory in a single detail: the son who is appointed does not sleep.$n$,38,1,1,'extras',94030),
('adam-eve-44-a-booth-and-seven-days-of-mourning',$n$A booth and seven days of mourning$n$,$n$Driven out of paradise, they build a booth and mourn: *they made themselves a booth, and spent seven days mourning and lamenting in great grief* (Adam and Eve 44:1). Genesis is short about the going out: *Therefore Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) sent him forth from the garden of Eden, to till the ground from whence he was taken.* (Genesis 3:23) What the man did with his first days outside, Moses does not say, and this book fills the silence with a house of branches and a week of weeping.

The weeping is not left without a direction. Joel hears Yahuah (the LORD) say what such weeping is for: *Therefore also now, saith Yahuah (LORD), turn ye even to me with all your heart, and with fasting, and with weeping, and with mourning:* (Joel 2:12) The call is not to a performance but to a return, with the whole heart. It ain't new that the road out of a garden and the road back both pass through grief. The canon does not make a rule of the booth; it puts beside it the call to turn, and the call is the part that stands.$n$,44,1,1,'extras',94035),
('adam-eve-45-hunger-and-the-hope-of-being-recalled',$n$Hunger, and the hope of being recalled$n$,$n$After seven days they are hungry and find nothing. Eve sends Adam to look: *Perchance Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) will look back and pity us and recall us to the place in which we were before* (Adam and Eve 45:1). The hunger is no accident of the wilderness; it is the sentence at work: *In the sweat of thy face shalt thou eat bread, till thou return unto the ground; for out of it wast thou taken: for dust thou art, and unto dust shalt thou return.* (Genesis 3:19) Bread by sweat was the shape of the new life, and Eve is standing at the first day of it.

But her word is not only about bread. She hopes to be looked back upon, and the psalm gives that hope a footing: *Like as a father pitieth his children, so Yahuah (LORD) pitieth them that fear him.* (Psalm 103:13) It ain't new that pity is the answer of Yahuah (the LORD) to those who fear him. This book leaves the question whether they will be recalled open, as Eve does; the canon says the Father does not wait for them to be worthy of his pity, only for them to turn toward him.$n$,45,1,1,'extras',94040),
('adam-eve-46-wilt-thou-slay-me',$n$Wilt thou slay me — and one flesh$n$,$n$Eve, in her grief, tells Adam that perhaps Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) would bring him into paradise if she were dead, since it was on her account that he was driven out. His answer is quick: *How is it possible that I should stretch forth my hand against my own flesh* (Adam and Eve 46:2). The answer rests on the first word of the garden, spoken when she was first brought to him: *And Adam said, This is now bone of my bones, and flesh of my flesh: she shall be called Woman, because she was taken out of Man.* (Genesis 2:23) What he says of her he says of himself.

Moses gives the reason in the verse after: *Therefore shall a man leave his father and his mother, and shall cleave unto his wife: and they shall be one flesh.* (Genesis 2:24) It ain't new that the two are one. The blame Eve takes on herself, and the blame Adam refused to carry in the garden, meet here in the plain refusal to let the household break. Adam turns the conversation to the one thing left to do, to arise and look for something to live on, that they fail not.$n$,46,1,2,'extras',94045),
('adam-eve-48-what-is-penitence',$n$What is penitence?$n$,$n$Eve asks what she has never been asked: *What is penitence Tell me, what sort of penitence am I to do* (Adam and Eve 48:1). She fears a labour that is too great to endure, and a promise they will not keep. The Preacher sees that fear and gives it a limit: *When thou vowest a vow unto Elohim (God), defer not to pay it; for he hath no pleasure in fools: pay that which thou hast vowed.* (Ecclesiastes 5:4) A vow made in grief is still a vow, and so Eve is right to be careful.

But the question of how much is not the one David answers. After his own fall he says what Elohim (God) wants: *The sacrifices of Elohim (God) are a broken spirit: a broken and a contrite heart, O Elohim (God), thou wilt not despise.* (Psalm 51:17) It ain't new that Yahuah (the LORD) looks first at the heart, and at the labour second. This book measures penitence in days in the water and fasting; the Psalm says what the days are meant to carry. A broken spirit is not a feat. It is a heart that has stopped defending itself.$n$,48,1,1,'extras',94050),
('adam-eve-53-the-devil-leads-eve-out-of-the-water',$n$The devil leads Eve out of the water$n$,$n$Eve hears, believes, and comes out of the water, trembling from the cold. Then: *the devil raised her up and led her to Adam* (Adam and Eve 53:1). Adam sees them together and weeps, and his cry is the cry of someone who has been here before: *How hast thou been again ensnared by our adversary* (Adam and Eve 53:2). Paul knew the pattern and set it down for the assemblies: *And no marvel; for Satan himself is transformed into an angel of light.* (2 Corinthians 11:14) The helper in this book is the same figure as the light in the letter.

The snare is not a thing the captive undoes alone. Paul speaks of the ones who *recover themselves out of the snare of the devil* (2 Timothy 2:26) and who are taken captive at his will. It ain't new that the deceiver comes as a friend, or that the captive can be recovered. This book sets it in a river with a woman who wanted to keep a promise, and the canon sets it in the assembly with those who hear the truth.$n$,53,1,2,'extras',94055),
('adam-eve-54-woe-unto-thee-devil',$n$Woe unto thee, devil — the rebuke$n$,$n$Eve sees who has led her out, falls on her face, and then cries: *Woe unto thee, thou devil. Why dost thou attack us for no cause* (Adam and Eve 54:1). It is a cry of the weak against the strong, and James knows its place in the walk: *Submit yourselves therefore to Elohim (God). Resist the devil, and he will flee from you.* (James 4:7) Submission comes before resistance, and the fleeing belongs to the one who submits.

Zechariah shows who the rebuke ultimately belongs to: *And Yahuah (LORD) said unto Satan, Yahuah (LORD) rebuke thee, O Satan; even Yahuah (LORD) that hath chosen Jerusalem rebuke thee: is not this a brand plucked out of the fire?* (Zechariah 3:2) It ain't new that the answer to the accuser comes from Yahuah (the LORD) and not from the accused. Eve's words are real and brave, and they are not the last word, for she is not able to send him away herself. The next scene shows the answer, when Adam cries to Yahuah (the Lord) and the devil vanishes.$n$,54,1,1,'extras',94060),
('adam-eve-55-expelled-from-my-glory',$n$Expelled from my glory — whose fault the fall was$n$,$n$With a heavy sigh the devil speaks to Adam: *it is for thee that I have been expelled from my glory* (Adam and Eve 55:1). Adam answers him as an honest man would, that he has done him no harm. The canon agrees with Adam. Ezekiel's word to the covering cherub gives the cause: *By the multitude of thy merchandise they have filled the midst of thee with violence, and thou hast sinned: therefore I will cast thee as profane out of the mountain of Elohim (God): and I will destroy thee, O covering cherub, from the midst of the stones of fire.* (Ezekiel 28:16) And the verse after names the root: *Thine heart was lifted up because of thy beauty, thou hast corrupted thy wisdom by reason of thy brightness: I will cast thee to the ground, I will lay thee before kings, that they may behold thee.* (Ezekiel 28:17)

This book ties the fall to the creation of Adam, and the prophet ties it to the heart that was lifted up. Yahusha (Jesus) saw it happen: *And he said unto them, I beheld Satan as lightning fall from heaven.* (Luke 10:18) It ain't new that the one who falls blames the one beside him. The devil's pursuit of Adam, in this book, is envy dressed as grievance; in Ezekiel it is plain pride, and no one else is held to answer for it.$n$,55,1,1,'extras',94065),
('adam-eve-60-my-life-is-in-thy-hands',$n$My life is in thy hands — forty days in the Jordan$n$,$n$When Adam hears the devil, he cries out and prays: *O Yahuah (Lord) my Elohim (God), my life is in thy hands. Banish this Adversary far from me* (Adam and Eve 60:1). The prayer is in the psalms already: *My times are in thy hand: deliver me from the hand of mine enemies, and from them that persecute me.* (Psalm 31:15) The man in the water and the king in his trouble ask the same thing from the same Hand.

The water matters too. Adam stands forty days in the Jordan, and the psalmist, who knows what it is to be overwhelmed, cries: *Save me, O Elohim (God); for the waters are come in unto my soul.* (Psalm 69:1) It ain't new that the one in trouble calls on Yahuah (the LORD) from inside it. The devil vanishes in this book at the moment of the prayer. The canon holds that the prayer of the one who submits is heard, and that the enemy has no standing against a man whose life is in the hands of Yahuah (the LORD).$n$,60,1,1,'extras',94070),
('adam-eve-70-let-not-the-name-be-blotted-out',$n$Let not the name be blotted out — the seed that serves$n$,$n$Adam hears the word of Elohim (God) and falls prone, praying: *Cast me not out from Thy presence* (Adam and Eve 70:1). David's prayer in the psalms is the same: *Cast me not away from thy presence; and take not thy holy spirit from me.* (Psalm 51:11) The man who has been cast out once asks not to be cast out again, and asks it for the sake of the name.

Then comes the promise: *there shall not be taken from thy seed for ever the (right) to serve Me* (Adam and Eve 70:2). Isaiah gives the promise its end: *For as the new heavens and the new earth, which I will make, shall remain before me, saith Yahuah (LORD), so shall your seed and your name remain.* (Isaiah 66:22) It ain't new that Yahuah (the LORD) keeps the seed. This book puts the promise in the mouth of the Voice to Adam, and Isaiah puts it at the new heavens and the new earth. The name and the seed are held by the One who made them, and neither is blotted out by the failure of one man.$n$,70,1,2,'extras',94075),
('adam-eve-71-the-true-light-and-the-living-life',$n$The true Light, the Living Life$n$,$n$Adam throws himself on the earth and adores: *Thou art the true Light gleaming above all light(s), the Living Life, infinite mighty Power* (Adam and Eve 71:2). The psalmist uses the same two words, light and life, for the same One: *For with thee is the fountain of life: in thy light shall we see light.* (Psalm 36:9) The fountain and the light are with Yahuah (the LORD), and the man who sees light sees it in His.

He also says how long this One has been: *Thou art the eternal and supreme Elohim (God)* (Adam and Eve 71:1). The prayer of Moses gives it: *Before the mountains were brought forth, or ever thou hadst formed the earth and the world, even from everlasting to everlasting, thou art Elohim (God).* (Psalm 90:2) It ain't new that the first man worships the One before the mountains. After his worship, the angel Michael seizes his hand and casts him out of paradise, and this book has the waters freeze at the touch of his rod. The praise comes before the leaving, and it does not ask anything back.$n$,71,1,3,'extras',94080),
('adam-eve-74-seth-would-go-to-the-gates-of-paradise',$n$Seth would go to the gates, and Adam says it is pain$n$,$n$Seth, thinking his father longs for the fruit of paradise, offers to go to the gates: *put dust on my head and throw myself down on the earth before the gates of paradise and lament* (Adam and Eve 74:1). The gesture is old in Yashar'el (Israel): *And Joshua rent his clothes, and fell to the earth upon his face before the ark of Yahuah (LORD) until the eventide, he and the elders of Yashar'el (Israel), and put dust upon their heads.* (Joshua 7:6) Dust and the face to the earth are the body's way of saying that nothing is owed.

Adam says he is not longing for fruit but is in pain, a word Seth does not know. He had not met it. David gives the word its place: *There is no soundness in my flesh because of thine anger; neither is there any rest in my bones because of my sin.* (Psalm 38:3) It ain't new that the flesh carries what the heart has done, and that Yahuah (the LORD) is the One it is brought to. In this retelling the pain is first named to a son who has never felt it, and that is how a father hands down the first lesson of a fallen world.$n$,74,1,2,'extras',94085),
('adam-eve-75-the-prohibition-and-the-two-portions',$n$The prohibition, and the portions of paradise$n$,$n$Adam begins his account to his sons, gathered to hear him: *he laid a prohibition on us concerning the tree of knowledge of good and evil, which is in the midst of paradise* (Adam and Eve 75:1). Moses has the same placing: *And out of the ground made Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) to grow every tree that is pleasant to the sight, and good for food; the tree of life also in the midst of the garden, and the tree of knowledge of good and evil.* (Genesis 2:9) Two trees stand in the middle, and one is to be left alone.

The command comes with a plain consequence: *But of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, thou shalt not eat of it: for in the day that thou eatest thereof thou shalt surely die.* (Genesis 2:17) It ain't new that the Word spoke clearly before the failure, so that neither of them could say they were not told. This book adds a detail the garden story does not carry, the parts of paradise given to Adam and to Eve, east and north to him, south and west to her. Moses gives the command and the trees and says nothing of portions. What the telling keeps is a father telling his sons, before he dies, what was said and what happened.$n$,75,1,1,'extras',94090),
('adam-eve-77-seventy-blows-upon-the-body',$n$Seventy blows upon the body, and upon all the race$n$,$n$Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) is angry, and says to Adam: *In that thou hast left behind my commandment and hast not kept my word* (Adam and Eve 77:1). The consequence is named at once: *seventy blows* (Adam and Eve 77:1) on every limb, and on all the race. The shape of it is not new. In the covenant, the judgement for leaving the word falls on the one who left it and on his seed: *Then Yahuah (LORD) will make thy plagues wonderful, and the plagues of thy seed, even great plagues, and of long continuance, and sore sicknesses, and of long continuance.* (Deuteronomy 28:59)

Paul states the reach of the first failure in a single sentence: *Wherefore, as by one man sin entered into the world, and death by sin; and so death passed upon all men, for that all have sinned:* (Romans 5:12) It ain't new that one man's act reaches his house. The Torah is not the curse here; the judgement is. The commandment was given, it was left, and the consequence came, which is the whole movement of Deuteronomy 28. This book counts the blows; the canon only says the plagues were sore, and long, and that they touched the seed.$n$,77,1,1,'extras',94095),
('adam-eve-86-six-days-and-herbs-of-fragrance',$n$Six days, and herbs of fragrance$n$,$n$Michael sends Seth back to his father: *the time of his life is fulfilled. Six days hence, his soul shall go off his body* (Adam and Eve 86:1). The span is the one Moses closes the book of Adam's life with: *And all the days that Adam lived were nine hundred and thirty years: and he died.* (Genesis 5:5) The man who was told in the day thou eatest thereof thou shalt surely die lives long, and then he dies, and the number is written down.

Eve and Seth return from the gates of paradise without the oil, carrying *herbs of fragrance, i.e. nard and crocus and calamus and cinnamon* (Adam and Eve 86:2). Those are four names from the Song: *Spikenard and saffron; calamus and cinnamon, with all trees of frankincense; myrrh and aloes, with all the chief spices:* (Song of Solomon 4:14) It ain't new that the spices of the garden were meant for the living to give each other. What they bring is what can be had outside, and the ending of the matter is on its way. The oil is not given now, as Michael said; the herbs are given, and the six days begin.$n$,86,1,2,'extras',94100),
('adam-eve-87-a-great-plague-for-all-our-generations',$n$A great plague for all our generations$n$,$n$Seth and his mother come back to Adam and tell him the beast bit Seth. Adam, in his sickness, hears it as the fall again: *A great plague hast thou brought upon us, transgression and sin for all our generations* (Adam and Eve 87:1). He even says what his children will say: they will curse them for the evils of the beginning. The reach is real. Paul writes it without softening: *For as in Adam all die, even so in Messiah (Christ) shall all be made alive.* (1 Corinthians 15:22)

The second half of the same verse is the answer this book does not reach here. And on the matter of the children's curse, Yahuah (the LORD) speaks through Ezekiel to a people who had a proverb of their own: *The soul that sinneth, it shall die. The son shall not bear the iniquity of the father, neither shall the father bear the iniquity of the son: the righteousness of the righteous shall be upon him, and the wickedness of the wicked shall be upon him.* (Ezekiel 18:20) It ain't new that sin spreads to the household and that each one is called to answer for his own. The grief of Adam is true, and the way out of it, in the canon, is the other half of that verse.$n$,87,1,1,'extras',94105),
('adam-eve-90-blessed-art-thou-for-pity-on-thy-creature',$n$Blessed art thou, O Yahuah, for pity on thy creature$n$,$n$The sentence is short: *Blessed art thou, O Yahuah (Lord), for thou hast had pity on Thy creature* (Adam and Eve 90:1). It is the blessing of someone who has been spared something he did not earn. The psalmist knows the ground of it: *Yahuah (LORD) is good to all: and his tender mercies are over all his works.* (Psalm 145:9)

The same word of blessing is heard again at the dawn of the gospels, on the lips of Zechariah as his son John is born: *Blessed be Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) of Yashar'el (Israel); for he hath visited and redeemed his people,* (Luke 1:68) It ain't new that the one who has been shown pity answers with blessing. This book puts it at the close of Adam's life; the canon puts it in the mouth of a priest at the beginning of a new visiting. The same Yahuah (the LORD) pities his creature at the first end and at the last.$n$,90,1,1,'extras',94110);
