INSERT INTO _s495_adam_eve_c VALUES
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',2,1,$n$And after this, Adam and Eve were with one another and while they were sleeping, Eve said to Adam her lord: 'My lord, Adam, behold, I have seen in a dream this night the blood of my son Amilabes who is styled Abel being poured into the mouth of Cain his brother and he went on drinking it without pity. But he begged him to leave him a little of it. Yet he hearkened not to him, but gulped down the whole; nor did it stay in his stomach, but came out of his mouth. And Adam said, 'Let us arise and go and see what has happened to them. (I fear) lest the adversary may be assailing them somewhere.'$n$,'canon','genesis',4,11,$n$And now art thou cursed from the earth, which hath opened her mouth to receive thy brother’s blood from thy hand;$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 4:11 — *And now art thou cursed from the earth, which hath opened her mouth to receive thy brother’s blood from thy hand;* *And after this, Adam and Eve were with one another and while they were sleeping, Eve said to Adam her lord: 'My lord, Adam, behold, I have seen in a dream this night the blood of my son Amilabes who is styled Abel being poured into the mouth of Cain his brother and he went on drinking it without pity. But he begged him to leave him a little of it. Yet he hearkened not to him, but gulped down the whole; nor did it stay in his stomach, but came out of his mouth. And Adam said, 'Let us arise and go and see what has happened to them. (I fear) lest the adversary may be assailing them somewhere.'* (Adam and Eve 2:1) Eve dreams the blood of Abel poured into Cain's mouth, and Moses gives the same picture from the ground's side, where the earth opens her mouth to take what Cain's hand shed. The dream shows the murder before it happens and Genesis tells it after, with the same blood and the same mouth.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',2,1,$n$And after this, Adam and Eve were with one another and while they were sleeping, Eve said to Adam her lord: 'My lord, Adam, behold, I have seen in a dream this night the blood of my son Amilabes who is styled Abel being poured into the mouth of Cain his brother and he went on drinking it without pity. But he begged him to leave him a little of it. Yet he hearkened not to him, but gulped down the whole; nor did it stay in his stomach, but came out of his mouth. And Adam said, 'Let us arise and go and see what has happened to them. (I fear) lest the adversary may be assailing them somewhere.'$n$,'canon','1-peter',5,8,$n$Be sober, be vigilant; because your adversary the devil, as a roaring lion, walketh about, seeking whom he may devour:$n$,'free',$n$1 Peter 5:8 — *Be sober, be vigilant; because your adversary the devil, as a roaring lion, walketh about, seeking whom he may devour:* *And after this, Adam and Eve were with one another and while they were sleeping, Eve said to Adam her lord: 'My lord, Adam, behold, I have seen in a dream this night the blood of my son Amilabes who is styled Abel being poured into the mouth of Cain his brother and he went on drinking it without pity. But he begged him to leave him a little of it. Yet he hearkened not to him, but gulped down the whole; nor did it stay in his stomach, but came out of his mouth. And Adam said, 'Let us arise and go and see what has happened to them. (I fear) lest the adversary may be assailing them somewhere.'* (Adam and Eve 2:1) Adam's fear as he rises is that the adversary may be at work against his sons, and Peter writes out of the same alarm. The adversary does not rest, and a father who knows it goes to look.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',6,1,$n$And Seth answered and said to him: 'Hast thou called to mind, father, the fruit of paradise of which thou usedst to eat, and hast been grieved in yearning for it' 'If this be so, tell me, (and) I will go and bring thee fruit from paradise. For I will set dung upon my head and will weep and pray that Yahuah (Lord) will hearken to me and send his angel (and bring me a plant from paradise), and I will bring it thee that thy trouble may cease from thee.' Adam saith to him: 'Nay, my son Seth, but I have (much) sickness and trouble!' Seth saith to him: 'And how hath this come upon thee'$n$,'canon','genesis',3,22,$n$And Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) said, Behold, the man is become as one of us, to know good and evil: and now, lest he put forth his hand, and take also of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever:$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 3:22 — *And Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) said, Behold, the man is become as one of us, to know good and evil: and now, lest he put forth his hand, and take also of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever:* *And Seth answered and said to him: 'Hast thou called to mind, father, the fruit of paradise of which thou usedst to eat, and hast been grieved in yearning for it' 'If this be so, tell me, (and) I will go and bring thee fruit from paradise. For I will set dung upon my head and will weep and pray that Yahuah (Lord) will hearken to me and send his angel (and bring me a plant from paradise), and I will bring it thee that thy trouble may cease from thee.' Adam saith to him: 'Nay, my son Seth, but I have (much) sickness and trouble!' Seth saith to him: 'And how hath this come upon thee'* (Adam and Eve 6:1) Seth is ready to go and bring the fruit, but Moses has already told why no son of Adam can simply go for it. The way to the tree of life was closed the day the man was sent out, lest he eat and live for ever, and Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) alone opens it again.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',6,1,$n$And Seth answered and said to him: 'Hast thou called to mind, father, the fruit of paradise of which thou usedst to eat, and hast been grieved in yearning for it' 'If this be so, tell me, (and) I will go and bring thee fruit from paradise. For I will set dung upon my head and will weep and pray that Yahuah (Lord) will hearken to me and send his angel (and bring me a plant from paradise), and I will bring it thee that thy trouble may cease from thee.' Adam saith to him: 'Nay, my son Seth, but I have (much) sickness and trouble!' Seth saith to him: 'And how hath this come upon thee'$n$,'canon','revelation',22,14,$n$Blessed are they that do his commandments, that they may have right to the tree of life, and may enter in through the gates into the city.$n$,'free',$n$Revelation 22:14 — *Blessed are they that do his commandments, that they may have right to the tree of life, and may enter in through the gates into the city.* *And Seth answered and said to him: 'Hast thou called to mind, father, the fruit of paradise of which thou usedst to eat, and hast been grieved in yearning for it' 'If this be so, tell me, (and) I will go and bring thee fruit from paradise. For I will set dung upon my head and will weep and pray that Yahuah (Lord) will hearken to me and send his angel (and bring me a plant from paradise), and I will bring it thee that thy trouble may cease from thee.' Adam saith to him: 'Nay, my son Seth, but I have (much) sickness and trouble!' Seth saith to him: 'And how hath this come upon thee'* (Adam and Eve 6:1) The last page of the canon returns to the same tree and says who may come to it: those who do his commandments. Seth's love for his father is good, and what the canon holds out is the return by the commandments, through the gates, to the tree of life.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',11,1,$n$'It is not our concern, Eve, thy greed and thy wailing, but thine own; for (it is) from thee that the rule of the beasts hath arisen. How was thy mouth opened to eat of the tree concerning which Elohim (God) enjoined thee not to eat of it On this account, our nature also hath been transformed. Now therefore thou canst not endure it, if I begin to reprove thee.'$n$,'canon','genesis',3,17,$n$And unto Adam he said, Because thou hast hearkened unto the voice of thy wife, and hast eaten of the tree, of which I commanded thee, saying, Thou shalt not eat of it: cursed is the ground for thy sake; in sorrow shalt thou eat of it all the days of thy life;$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 3:17 — *And unto Adam he said, Because thou hast hearkened unto the voice of thy wife, and hast eaten of the tree, of which I commanded thee, saying, Thou shalt not eat of it: cursed is the ground for thy sake; in sorrow shalt thou eat of it all the days of thy life;* *'It is not our concern, Eve, thy greed and thy wailing, but thine own; for (it is) from thee that the rule of the beasts hath arisen. How was thy mouth opened to eat of the tree concerning which Elohim (God) enjoined thee not to eat of it On this account, our nature also hath been transformed. Now therefore thou canst not endure it, if I begin to reprove thee.'* (Adam and Eve 11:1) Adam reproves Eve for opening her mouth to eat of the tree Elohim (God) forbade, and the reproof is true as far as it goes. Moses records the word of Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) to Adam himself, and it lays the weight on the man as well, because he hearkened and he ate.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',11,1,$n$'It is not our concern, Eve, thy greed and thy wailing, but thine own; for (it is) from thee that the rule of the beasts hath arisen. How was thy mouth opened to eat of the tree concerning which Elohim (God) enjoined thee not to eat of it On this account, our nature also hath been transformed. Now therefore thou canst not endure it, if I begin to reprove thee.'$n$,'canon','genesis',3,12,$n$And the man said, The woman whom thou gavest to be with me, she gave me of the tree, and I did eat.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 3:12 — *And the man said, The woman whom thou gavest to be with me, she gave me of the tree, and I did eat.* *'It is not our concern, Eve, thy greed and thy wailing, but thine own; for (it is) from thee that the rule of the beasts hath arisen. How was thy mouth opened to eat of the tree concerning which Elohim (God) enjoined thee not to eat of it On this account, our nature also hath been transformed. Now therefore thou canst not endure it, if I begin to reprove thee.'* (Adam and Eve 11:1) Adam's pointing at Eve here is the same hand Moses shows in the garden, when he answers that the woman whom thou gavest me gave him of the tree. This book carries the old habit forward, and Genesis lets the reader see it for what it was.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',15,1,$n$Then saith Eve to them: 'Hear all my children and children's children and I will relate to you how the enemy deceived us. It befell that we were guarding paradise, each of us the portion allotted to us from Elohim (God). Now I guarded in my lot, the west and the south. But the devil went to Adam's lot, where the male creatures were. [For Elohim (God) divided the creatures; all the males he gave to your father and all the females he gave to me.]$n$,'canon','genesis',2,15,$n$And Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) took the man, and put him into the garden of Eden to dress it and to keep it.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 2:15 — *And Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) took the man, and put him into the garden of Eden to dress it and to keep it.* *Then saith Eve to them: 'Hear all my children and children's children and I will relate to you how the enemy deceived us. It befell that we were guarding paradise, each of us the portion allotted to us from Elohim (God). Now I guarded in my lot, the west and the south. But the devil went to Adam's lot, where the male creatures were. [For Elohim (God) divided the creatures; all the males he gave to your father and all the females he gave to me.]* (Adam and Eve 15:1) Eve tells her children that she and Adam were guarding paradise, each in an allotted portion. Moses says it more briefly: Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) put the man in the garden to dress it and to keep it. The keeping was given to the man as work, and this book remembers it as a watch.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',15,1,$n$Then saith Eve to them: 'Hear all my children and children's children and I will relate to you how the enemy deceived us. It befell that we were guarding paradise, each of us the portion allotted to us from Elohim (God). Now I guarded in my lot, the west and the south. But the devil went to Adam's lot, where the male creatures were. [For Elohim (God) divided the creatures; all the males he gave to your father and all the females he gave to me.]$n$,'canon','genesis',2,19,$n$And out of the ground Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) formed every beast of the field, and every fowl of the air; and brought them unto Adam to see what he would call them: and whatsoever Adam called every living creature, that was the name thereof.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 2:19 — *And out of the ground Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) formed every beast of the field, and every fowl of the air; and brought them unto Adam to see what he would call them: and whatsoever Adam called every living creature, that was the name thereof.* *Then saith Eve to them: 'Hear all my children and children's children and I will relate to you how the enemy deceived us. It befell that we were guarding paradise, each of us the portion allotted to us from Elohim (God). Now I guarded in my lot, the west and the south. But the devil went to Adam's lot, where the male creatures were. [For Elohim (God) divided the creatures; all the males he gave to your father and all the females he gave to me.]* (Adam and Eve 15:1) Eve says the creatures were divided, the males given to Adam and the females to her. Genesis says only that the beasts and fowl were brought to Adam to see what he would call them. The bracketed line about dividing the creatures is not found in Moses, and the reader sees what the first telling holds and what a later hand added.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',16,2,$n$"I hear that thou art wiser than all the beasts, and I have come to counsel thee. Why dost thou eat of Adam's tares and not of paradise Rise up and we will cause him to be cast out of paradise, even as we were cast out through him." The serpent saith to him, "I fear lest Yahuah (Lord) be wroth with me." The devil saith to him: "Fear not, only be my vessel and I will speak through thy mouth words to deceive him."$n$,'canon','genesis',3,1,$n$Now the serpent was more subtil than any beast of the field which Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) had made. And he said unto the woman, Yea, hath Elohim (God) said, Ye shall not eat of every tree of the garden?$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 3:1 — *Now the serpent was more subtil than any beast of the field which Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) had made. And he said unto the woman, Yea, hath Elohim (God) said, Ye shall not eat of every tree of the garden?* *"I hear that thou art wiser than all the beasts, and I have come to counsel thee. Why dost thou eat of Adam's tares and not of paradise Rise up and we will cause him to be cast out of paradise, even as we were cast out through him." The serpent saith to him, "I fear lest Yahuah (Lord) be wroth with me." The devil saith to him: "Fear not, only be my vessel and I will speak through thy mouth words to deceive him."* (Adam and Eve 16:2) The devil tells the serpent he will speak through his mouth words to deceive Adam, and Moses shows those words arriving. The serpent, more subtil than any beast, asks the woman a question, and the question in the garden had a speaker behind it.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',16,1,$n$And the devil spake to the serpent saying, Rise up, come to me and I will tell thee a word whereby thou mayst have profit." And he arose and came to him. And the devil saith to him:$n$,'canon','revelation',12,9,$n$And the great dragon was cast out, that old serpent, called the Devil, and Satan, which deceiveth the whole world: he was cast out into the earth, and his angels were cast out with him.$n$,'free',$n$Revelation 12:9 — *And the great dragon was cast out, that old serpent, called the Devil, and Satan, which deceiveth the whole world: he was cast out into the earth, and his angels were cast out with him.* *And the devil spake to the serpent saying, Rise up, come to me and I will tell thee a word whereby thou mayst have profit." And he arose and came to him. And the devil saith to him:* (Adam and Eve 16:1) Here the devil rises and counsels the serpent as two. John names them as one: the great dragon, that old serpent, called the Devil, and Satan. The serpent of Genesis is not a mere beast, and the deceiver who was cast out into the earth is the one who comes to the garden.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',27,1,$n$Thus he spake and bade the angels have us cast out of paradise: and as we were being driven out amid our loud lamentations, your father Adam besought the angels and said: "Leave me a little (space) that I may entreat Yahuah (Lord) that he have compassion on me and pity me, for I only have sinned." And they left off driving him and Adam cried aloud and wept saying: "Pardon me O Yahuah (Lord), my deed." Then Yahuah (Lord) saith to the angels, "Why have ye ceased from driving Adam from paradise Why do ye not cast him out Is it I who have done wrong Or is my judgement badly judged" Then the angels fell down on the ground and worshipped Yahuah (Lord) saying, "Thou art just, O Yahuah (Lord), and thou judgest righteous judgement."$n$,'canon','psalms',119,137,$n$Righteous art thou, O Yahuah (LORD), and upright are thy judgments.$n$,'free',$n$Psalm 119:137 — *Righteous art thou, O Yahuah (LORD), and upright are thy judgments.* *Thus he spake and bade the angels have us cast out of paradise: and as we were being driven out amid our loud lamentations, your father Adam besought the angels and said: "Leave me a little (space) that I may entreat Yahuah (Lord) that he have compassion on me and pity me, for I only have sinned." And they left off driving him and Adam cried aloud and wept saying: "Pardon me O Yahuah (Lord), my deed." Then Yahuah (Lord) saith to the angels, "Why have ye ceased from driving Adam from paradise Why do ye not cast him out Is it I who have done wrong Or is my judgement badly judged" Then the angels fell down on the ground and worshipped Yahuah (Lord) saying, "Thou art just, O Yahuah (Lord), and thou judgest righteous judgement."* (Adam and Eve 27:1) The angels fall down and say Thou art just, O Yahuah (Lord), and the psalmist says it with his own mouth: Righteous art thou, O Yahuah (LORD), and upright are thy judgments. Adam's plea is heard, and still the judgment is not called wrong by anyone who stands in the presence of Elohim (God).$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',27,1,$n$Thus he spake and bade the angels have us cast out of paradise: and as we were being driven out amid our loud lamentations, your father Adam besought the angels and said: "Leave me a little (space) that I may entreat Yahuah (Lord) that he have compassion on me and pity me, for I only have sinned." And they left off driving him and Adam cried aloud and wept saying: "Pardon me O Yahuah (Lord), my deed." Then Yahuah (Lord) saith to the angels, "Why have ye ceased from driving Adam from paradise Why do ye not cast him out Is it I who have done wrong Or is my judgement badly judged" Then the angels fell down on the ground and worshipped Yahuah (Lord) saying, "Thou art just, O Yahuah (Lord), and thou judgest righteous judgement."$n$,'canon','genesis',3,24,$n$So he drove out the man; and he placed at the east of the garden of Eden Cherubims, and a flaming sword which turned every way, to keep the way of the tree of life.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 3:24 — *So he drove out the man; and he placed at the east of the garden of Eden Cherubims, and a flaming sword which turned every way, to keep the way of the tree of life.* *Thus he spake and bade the angels have us cast out of paradise: and as we were being driven out amid our loud lamentations, your father Adam besought the angels and said: "Leave me a little (space) that I may entreat Yahuah (Lord) that he have compassion on me and pity me, for I only have sinned." And they left off driving him and Adam cried aloud and wept saying: "Pardon me O Yahuah (Lord), my deed." Then Yahuah (Lord) saith to the angels, "Why have ye ceased from driving Adam from paradise Why do ye not cast him out Is it I who have done wrong Or is my judgement badly judged" Then the angels fell down on the ground and worshipped Yahuah (Lord) saying, "Thou art just, O Yahuah (Lord), and thou judgest righteous judgement."* (Adam and Eve 27:1) Moses gives the plain end of the scene: he drove out the man and placed cherubim and a flaming sword at the east of the garden. This book gives Adam a moment to plead, and the judgement still stands; the canon records the door shut and kept.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',30,1,$n$Now then, my children, I have shown you the way in which we were deceived; and do ye guard yourselves from transgressing against the good.'$n$,'canon','deuteronomy',4,9,$n$Only take heed to thyself, and keep thy soul diligently, lest thou forget the things which thine eyes have seen, and lest they depart from thy heart all the days of thy life: but teach them thy sons, and thy sons’ sons;$n$,'free',$n$Deuteronomy 4:9 — *Only take heed to thyself, and keep thy soul diligently, lest thou forget the things which thine eyes have seen, and lest they depart from thy heart all the days of thy life: but teach them thy sons, and thy sons’ sons;* *Now then, my children, I have shown you the way in which we were deceived; and do ye guard yourselves from transgressing against the good.'* (Adam and Eve 30:1) Eve ends her telling by warning her children and her children's children to guard themselves. Moses gives Yashar'el (Israel) the same charge about what their eyes have seen: keep it, and teach it to sons and sons' sons. A lesson that is not handed down is lost.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',30,1,$n$Now then, my children, I have shown you the way in which we were deceived; and do ye guard yourselves from transgressing against the good.'$n$,'canon','genesis',3,13,$n$And Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) said unto the woman, What is this that thou hast done? And the woman said, The serpent beguiled me, and I did eat.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 3:13 — *And Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) said unto the woman, What is this that thou hast done? And the woman said, The serpent beguiled me, and I did eat.* *Now then, my children, I have shown you the way in which we were deceived; and do ye guard yourselves from transgressing against the good.'* (Adam and Eve 30:1) Eve has just shown them how they were deceived. In Genesis her own answer to Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) is the shortest account of it: The serpent beguiled me, and I did eat. The long telling and the one line say the same thing.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',34,1,$n$And I Eve beheld two great and fearful wonders standing in the presence of Elohim (God) and I wept for fear, and I cried aloud to my son Seth and said, 'Rise up, Seth, from the body of thy father Adam and come to me, and thou shalt see a spectacle which no man's eye hath yet beheld.'$n$,'canon','psalms',148,3,$n$Praise ye him, sun and moon: praise him, all ye stars of light.$n$,'free',$n$Psalm 148:3 — *Praise ye him, sun and moon: praise him, all ye stars of light.* *And I Eve beheld two great and fearful wonders standing in the presence of Elohim (God) and I wept for fear, and I cried aloud to my son Seth and said, 'Rise up, Seth, from the body of thy father Adam and come to me, and thou shalt see a spectacle which no man's eye hath yet beheld.'* (Adam and Eve 34:1) Eve sees two great and fearful wonders standing in the presence of Elohim (God), and the next chapters name them as the sun and the moon. The psalmist calls the same two to the same place: Praise ye him, sun and moon. The lights stand before Elohim (God) and answer to him.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',34,1,$n$And I Eve beheld two great and fearful wonders standing in the presence of Elohim (God) and I wept for fear, and I cried aloud to my son Seth and said, 'Rise up, Seth, from the body of thy father Adam and come to me, and thou shalt see a spectacle which no man's eye hath yet beheld.'$n$,'canon','genesis',1,16,$n$And Elohim (God) made two great lights; the greater light to rule the day, and the lesser light to rule the night: he made the stars also.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 1:16 — *And Elohim (God) made two great lights; the greater light to rule the day, and the lesser light to rule the night: he made the stars also.* *And I Eve beheld two great and fearful wonders standing in the presence of Elohim (God) and I wept for fear, and I cried aloud to my son Seth and said, 'Rise up, Seth, from the body of thy father Adam and come to me, and thou shalt see a spectacle which no man's eye hath yet beheld.'* (Adam and Eve 34:1) Moses tells how the two great lights came to be, the greater to rule the day and the lesser the night. The lights Eve sees fearful in the presence of Elohim (God) are the lights he made, and they are servants and not rulers of anything beyond what he gave.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',35,1,$n$Then Seth arose and came to his mother and to her he saith: 'What is thy trouble Why weepest thou' (And) she saith to him: 'Look up and see with thine eyes the seven heavens opened, and see how the soul of thy father lies on its face and all the holy angels are praying on his behalf and saying: 'Pardon him, Father of All, for he is Thine image." 'Pray, my child Seth, what shall this mean And will he one day be delivered into the hands of the Invisible Father, even our Elohim (God) But who are the two negroes who stand by at the prayers for thy father Adam'$n$,'canon','genesis',1,27,$n$So Elohim (God) created man in his own image, in the image of Elohim (God) created he him; male and female created he them.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 1:27 — *So Elohim (God) created man in his own image, in the image of Elohim (God) created he him; male and female created he them.* *Then Seth arose and came to his mother and to her he saith: 'What is thy trouble Why weepest thou' (And) she saith to him: 'Look up and see with thine eyes the seven heavens opened, and see how the soul of thy father lies on its face and all the holy angels are praying on his behalf and saying: 'Pardon him, Father of All, for he is Thine image." 'Pray, my child Seth, what shall this mean And will he one day be delivered into the hands of the Invisible Father, even our Elohim (God) But who are the two negroes who stand by at the prayers for thy father Adam'* (Adam and Eve 35:1) The angels pray for Adam's soul and say Pardon him, for he is Thine image. Moses is the source of that word, for Elohim (God) created man in his own image, and the image is the reason anyone should plead for him.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',35,1,$n$Then Seth arose and came to his mother and to her he saith: 'What is thy trouble Why weepest thou' (And) she saith to him: 'Look up and see with thine eyes the seven heavens opened, and see how the soul of thy father lies on its face and all the holy angels are praying on his behalf and saying: 'Pardon him, Father of All, for he is Thine image." 'Pray, my child Seth, what shall this mean And will he one day be delivered into the hands of the Invisible Father, even our Elohim (God) But who are the two negroes who stand by at the prayers for thy father Adam'$n$,'canon','ezekiel',1,1,$n$Now it came to pass in the thirtieth year, in the fourth month, in the fifth day of the month, as I was among the captives by the river of Chebar, that the heavens were opened, and I saw visions of Elohim (God).$n$,'free',$n$Ezekiel 1:1 — *Now it came to pass in the thirtieth year, in the fourth month, in the fifth day of the month, as I was among the captives by the river of Chebar, that the heavens were opened, and I saw visions of Elohim (God).* *Then Seth arose and came to his mother and to her he saith: 'What is thy trouble Why weepest thou' (And) she saith to him: 'Look up and see with thine eyes the seven heavens opened, and see how the soul of thy father lies on its face and all the holy angels are praying on his behalf and saying: 'Pardon him, Father of All, for he is Thine image." 'Pray, my child Seth, what shall this mean And will he one day be delivered into the hands of the Invisible Father, even our Elohim (God) But who are the two negroes who stand by at the prayers for thy father Adam'* (Adam and Eve 35:1) Eve tells Seth to look up and see the seven heavens opened. Ezekiel, by the river of Chebar among the captives, says the same of the moment he was shown: the heavens were opened, and I saw visions of Elohim (God). What is seen in such a moment is given; it is not gone and fetched.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',38,1,$n$But after all this, the archangel asked concerning the laying out of the remains. And Elohim (God) commanded that all the angels should assemble in His presence, each in his order, and all the angels assembled, some having censers in their hands, and others trumpets. And lo ! the 'Lord of Hosts' came on and four winds drew Him and cherubim mounted on the winds and the angels from heaven escorting Him and they came on the earth, where was the body of Adam. And they came to paradise and all the leaves of paradise were stirred so that all men begotten of Adam slept from the fragrance save Seth alone, because he was born 'according to the appointment of Elohim (God) '. Then Adam's body lay there in paradise on the earth and Seth grieved exceedingly over him.$n$,'canon','psalms',18,10,$n$And he rode upon a cherub, and did fly: yea, he did fly upon the wings of the wind.$n$,'free',$n$Psalm 18:10 — *And he rode upon a cherub, and did fly: yea, he did fly upon the wings of the wind.* *But after all this, the archangel asked concerning the laying out of the remains. And Elohim (God) commanded that all the angels should assemble in His presence, each in his order, and all the angels assembled, some having censers in their hands, and others trumpets. And lo ! the 'Lord of Hosts' came on and four winds drew Him and cherubim mounted on the winds and the angels from heaven escorting Him and they came on the earth, where was the body of Adam. And they came to paradise and all the leaves of paradise were stirred so that all men begotten of Adam slept from the fragrance save Seth alone, because he was born 'according to the appointment of Elohim (God) '. Then Adam's body lay there in paradise on the earth and Seth grieved exceedingly over him.* (Adam and Eve 38:1) Yahuah Tseva'ot (the Lord of Hosts) comes down drawn by four winds, with cherubim mounted on the winds. David sings the same image in plain words: he rode upon a cherub, and did fly: yea, he did fly upon the wings of the wind. This book dresses what the psalm gives.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',38,1,$n$But after all this, the archangel asked concerning the laying out of the remains. And Elohim (God) commanded that all the angels should assemble in His presence, each in his order, and all the angels assembled, some having censers in their hands, and others trumpets. And lo ! the 'Lord of Hosts' came on and four winds drew Him and cherubim mounted on the winds and the angels from heaven escorting Him and they came on the earth, where was the body of Adam. And they came to paradise and all the leaves of paradise were stirred so that all men begotten of Adam slept from the fragrance save Seth alone, because he was born 'according to the appointment of Elohim (God) '. Then Adam's body lay there in paradise on the earth and Seth grieved exceedingly over him.$n$,'canon','genesis',4,25,$n$And Adam knew his wife again; and she bare a son, and called his name Seth: For Elohim (God), said she, hath appointed me another seed instead of Abel, whom Cain slew.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 4:25 — *And Adam knew his wife again; and she bare a son, and called his name Seth: For Elohim (God), said she, hath appointed me another seed instead of Abel, whom Cain slew.* *But after all this, the archangel asked concerning the laying out of the remains. And Elohim (God) commanded that all the angels should assemble in His presence, each in his order, and all the angels assembled, some having censers in their hands, and others trumpets. And lo ! the 'Lord of Hosts' came on and four winds drew Him and cherubim mounted on the winds and the angels from heaven escorting Him and they came on the earth, where was the body of Adam. And they came to paradise and all the leaves of paradise were stirred so that all men begotten of Adam slept from the fragrance save Seth alone, because he was born 'according to the appointment of Elohim (God) '. Then Adam's body lay there in paradise on the earth and Seth grieved exceedingly over him.* (Adam and Eve 38:1) All the sons of Adam sleep from the fragrance but Seth alone, because he was born according to the appointment of Elohim (God). Moses gives the appointing in Eve's own words: Elohim (God) hath appointed me another seed instead of Abel, whom Cain slew. The line of promise runs through the son appointed, and this book remembers it.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',44,1,$n$When they were driven out from paradise, they made themselves a booth, and spent seven days mourning and lamenting in great grief.$n$,'canon','genesis',3,23,$n$Therefore Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) sent him forth from the garden of Eden, to till the ground from whence he was taken.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 3:23 — *Therefore Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) sent him forth from the garden of Eden, to till the ground from whence he was taken.* *When they were driven out from paradise, they made themselves a booth, and spent seven days mourning and lamenting in great grief.* (Adam and Eve 44:1) Cast out, the first two make a booth and sit seven days in grief. Moses says only that Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) sent him forth to till the ground from whence he was taken. The weeping is this book's, and a likely one.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',44,1,$n$When they were driven out from paradise, they made themselves a booth, and spent seven days mourning and lamenting in great grief.$n$,'canon','joel',2,12,$n$Therefore also now, saith Yahuah (LORD), turn ye even to me with all your heart, and with fasting, and with weeping, and with mourning:$n$,'free',$n$Joel 2:12 — *Therefore also now, saith Yahuah (LORD), turn ye even to me with all your heart, and with fasting, and with weeping, and with mourning:* *When they were driven out from paradise, they made themselves a booth, and spent seven days mourning and lamenting in great grief.* (Adam and Eve 44:1) The call of Yahuah (the LORD) to a people gone away is to turn to him with all the heart, with fasting, with weeping, and with mourning. The seven days of grief in the booth lie on that road, a grief that is already turning back toward him.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',45,1,$n$But after seven days, they began to be hungry and started to look for victual to eat, and they found it not. Then Eve said to Adam: 'My lord, I am hungry. Go, look for (something) for us to eat. Perchance Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) will look back and pity us and recall us to the place in which we were before.$n$,'canon','genesis',3,19,$n$In the sweat of thy face shalt thou eat bread, till thou return unto the ground; for out of it wast thou taken: for dust thou art, and unto dust shalt thou return.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 3:19 — *In the sweat of thy face shalt thou eat bread, till thou return unto the ground; for out of it wast thou taken: for dust thou art, and unto dust shalt thou return.* *But after seven days, they began to be hungry and started to look for victual to eat, and they found it not. Then Eve said to Adam: 'My lord, I am hungry. Go, look for (something) for us to eat. Perchance Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) will look back and pity us and recall us to the place in which we were before.* (Adam and Eve 45:1) Hunger comes straight out of the sentence. In the sweat of thy face shalt thou eat bread, Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) had said, and Eve's cry that she is hungry and finds nothing to eat is the first taste of it.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',45,1,$n$But after seven days, they began to be hungry and started to look for victual to eat, and they found it not. Then Eve said to Adam: 'My lord, I am hungry. Go, look for (something) for us to eat. Perchance Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) will look back and pity us and recall us to the place in which we were before.$n$,'canon','psalms',103,13,$n$Like as a father pitieth his children, so Yahuah (LORD) pitieth them that fear him.$n$,'free',$n$Psalm 103:13 — *Like as a father pitieth his children, so Yahuah (LORD) pitieth them that fear him.* *But after seven days, they began to be hungry and started to look for victual to eat, and they found it not. Then Eve said to Adam: 'My lord, I am hungry. Go, look for (something) for us to eat. Perchance Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) will look back and pity us and recall us to the place in which we were before.* (Adam and Eve 45:1) Eve hopes that Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) will look back and pity them and recall them to the place they were. The psalm grants the hope its ground: like a father with his children, Yahuah (the LORD) pities those that fear him.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',46,2,$n$Adam answered: 'Forbear, Eve, from such words, that peradventure Elohim (God) bring not some other curse upon us. How is it possible that I should stretch forth my hand against my own flesh Nay, let us arise and look for something for us to live on, that we fail not.'$n$,'canon','genesis',2,23,$n$And Adam said, This is now bone of my bones, and flesh of my flesh: she shall be called Woman, because she was taken out of Man.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 2:23 — *And Adam said, This is now bone of my bones, and flesh of my flesh: she shall be called Woman, because she was taken out of Man.* *Adam answered: 'Forbear, Eve, from such words, that peradventure Elohim (God) bring not some other curse upon us. How is it possible that I should stretch forth my hand against my own flesh Nay, let us arise and look for something for us to live on, that we fail not.'* (Adam and Eve 46:2) Eve says, Wilt thou slay me, and Adam answers that he cannot stretch forth his hand against his own flesh. Moses has the first words the man spoke of her: bone of my bones, and flesh of my flesh. What he could not do to her he could not do to himself.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',46,2,$n$Adam answered: 'Forbear, Eve, from such words, that peradventure Elohim (God) bring not some other curse upon us. How is it possible that I should stretch forth my hand against my own flesh Nay, let us arise and look for something for us to live on, that we fail not.'$n$,'canon','genesis',2,24,$n$Therefore shall a man leave his father and his mother, and shall cleave unto his wife: and they shall be one flesh.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 2:24 — *Therefore shall a man leave his father and his mother, and shall cleave unto his wife: and they shall be one flesh.* *Adam answered: 'Forbear, Eve, from such words, that peradventure Elohim (God) bring not some other curse upon us. How is it possible that I should stretch forth my hand against my own flesh Nay, let us arise and look for something for us to live on, that we fail not.'* (Adam and Eve 46:2) The man and the woman are one flesh in Genesis, and that is the weight under Adam's answer. He will go on seeking something to live on with her, not apart from her.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',48,1,$n$And Eve said to Adam: 'What is penitence Tell me, what sort of penitence am I to do Let us not put too great a labour on ourselves, which we cannot endure, so that Yahuah (Lord) will not hearken to our prayers: and will turn away His countenance from us, because we have not fulfilled what we promised. My lord, how much penitence hast thou thought (to do) for I have brought trouble and anguish upon thee'$n$,'canon','ecclesiastes',5,4,$n$When thou vowest a vow unto Elohim (God), defer not to pay it; for he hath no pleasure in fools: pay that which thou hast vowed.$n$,'free',$n$Ecclesiastes 5:4 — *When thou vowest a vow unto Elohim (God), defer not to pay it; for he hath no pleasure in fools: pay that which thou hast vowed.* *And Eve said to Adam: 'What is penitence Tell me, what sort of penitence am I to do Let us not put too great a labour on ourselves, which we cannot endure, so that Yahuah (Lord) will not hearken to our prayers: and will turn away His countenance from us, because we have not fulfilled what we promised. My lord, how much penitence hast thou thought (to do) for I have brought trouble and anguish upon thee'* (Adam and Eve 48:1) Eve fears that Yahuah (the Lord) will turn away because they have not fulfilled what they promised. The Preacher says the same weighty thing: when thou vowest a vow unto Elohim (God), defer not to pay it. A promise made in distress is still a promise.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',48,1,$n$And Eve said to Adam: 'What is penitence Tell me, what sort of penitence am I to do Let us not put too great a labour on ourselves, which we cannot endure, so that Yahuah (Lord) will not hearken to our prayers: and will turn away His countenance from us, because we have not fulfilled what we promised. My lord, how much penitence hast thou thought (to do) for I have brought trouble and anguish upon thee'$n$,'canon','psalms',51,17,$n$The sacrifices of Elohim (God) are a broken spirit: a broken and a contrite heart, O Elohim (God), thou wilt not despise.$n$,'free',$n$Psalm 51:17 — *The sacrifices of Elohim (God) are a broken spirit: a broken and a contrite heart, O Elohim (God), thou wilt not despise.* *And Eve said to Adam: 'What is penitence Tell me, what sort of penitence am I to do Let us not put too great a labour on ourselves, which we cannot endure, so that Yahuah (Lord) will not hearken to our prayers: and will turn away His countenance from us, because we have not fulfilled what we promised. My lord, how much penitence hast thou thought (to do) for I have brought trouble and anguish upon thee'* (Adam and Eve 48:1) She asks what penitence is, and what labour is enough. David's answer after his own fall is that Elohim (God) does not ask a measured labour but a broken and contrite heart.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',50,1,$n$And Eve walked to the river Tigris and did as Adam had told her. Likewise, Adam walked to the river Jordan and stood on a stone up to his neck in water.$n$,'canon','genesis',2,14,$n$And the name of the third river is Hiddekel: that is it which goeth toward the east of Assyria. And the fourth river is Euphrates.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 2:14 — *And the name of the third river is Hiddekel: that is it which goeth toward the east of Assyria. And the fourth river is Euphrates.* *And Eve walked to the river Tigris and did as Adam had told her. Likewise, Adam walked to the river Jordan and stood on a stone up to his neck in water.* (Adam and Eve 50:1) Eve walks to the river Tigris. Moses names the third river of Eden Hiddekel, which goeth toward the east of Assyria, and that is the river now called the Tigris. She stands in water that left Eden.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',50,1,$n$And Eve walked to the river Tigris and did as Adam had told her. Likewise, Adam walked to the river Jordan and stood on a stone up to his neck in water.$n$,'canon','matthew',3,6,$n$And were baptized of him in Jordan, confessing their sins.$n$,'free',$n$Matthew 3:6 — *And were baptized of him in Jordan, confessing their sins.* *And Eve walked to the river Tigris and did as Adam had told her. Likewise, Adam walked to the river Jordan and stood on a stone up to his neck in water.* (Adam and Eve 50:1) Adam stands in the Jordan, and those who came to John went down into the same river, baptized, confessing their sins. The Jordan is the river the people later went down into to confess, and this book has Adam standing in it first.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',53,1,$n$But Eve heard and believed and went out of the water of the river, and her flesh was (trembling) like grass, from the chill of the water. And when she had gone out, she fell on the earth and the devil raised her up and led her to Adam.$n$,'canon','2-corinthians',11,14,$n$And no marvel; for Satan himself is transformed into an angel of light.$n$,'free',$n$2 Corinthians 11:14 — *And no marvel; for Satan himself is transformed into an angel of light.* *But Eve heard and believed and went out of the water of the river, and her flesh was (trembling) like grass, from the chill of the water. And when she had gone out, she fell on the earth and the devil raised her up and led her to Adam.* (Adam and Eve 53:1) The devil comes to Eve as helper, lifts her out of the water and leads her to Adam. Paul says there is no marvel in it, for Satan himself is transformed into an angel of light. The one who looks like help is not always help.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',53,2,$n$But when Adam had seen her and the devil with her, he wept and cried aloud and said: 'O Eve, Eve, where is the labour of thy penitence How hast thou been again ensnared by our adversary, by whose means we have been estranged from our abode in paradise and spiritual joy'$n$,'canon','2-timothy',2,26,$n$And that they may recover themselves out of the snare of the devil, who are taken captive by him at his will.$n$,'free',$n$2 Timothy 2:26 — *And that they may recover themselves out of the snare of the devil, who are taken captive by him at his will.* *But when Adam had seen her and the devil with her, he wept and cried aloud and said: 'O Eve, Eve, where is the labour of thy penitence How hast thou been again ensnared by our adversary, by whose means we have been estranged from our abode in paradise and spiritual joy'* (Adam and Eve 53:2) Adam's cry, how hast thou been again ensnared by our adversary, is the cry of one who knows a snare. Paul says there is a way out of it: they may recover themselves out of the snare of the devil, who are taken captive by him at his will.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',54,1,$n$And when she heard this, Eve understood that (it was) the devil (who) had persuaded her to go out of the river; and she fell on her face on the earth and her sorrow and groaning and wailing was redoubled. And she cried out and said: 'Woe unto thee, thou devil. Why dost thou attack us for no cause What hast thou to do with us What have we done to thee for thou pursuest us with craft Or why doth thy malice assail us Have we taken away thy glory and caused thee to be without honour Why dost thou harry us, thou enemy (and persecute us) to the death in wickedness and envy'$n$,'canon','james',4,7,$n$Submit yourselves therefore to Elohim (God). Resist the devil, and he will flee from you.$n$,'free',$n$James 4:7 — *Submit yourselves therefore to Elohim (God). Resist the devil, and he will flee from you.* *And when she heard this, Eve understood that (it was) the devil (who) had persuaded her to go out of the river; and she fell on her face on the earth and her sorrow and groaning and wailing was redoubled. And she cried out and said: 'Woe unto thee, thou devil. Why dost thou attack us for no cause What hast thou to do with us What have we done to thee for thou pursuest us with craft Or why doth thy malice assail us Have we taken away thy glory and caused thee to be without honour Why dost thou harry us, thou enemy (and persecute us) to the death in wickedness and envy'* (Adam and Eve 54:1) Eve turns on the devil and cries out against him, and the order James gives is submit first, then resist. The flight of the devil comes in a later scene, after Adam cries to Yahuah (the Lord) in the water.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',54,1,$n$And when she heard this, Eve understood that (it was) the devil (who) had persuaded her to go out of the river; and she fell on her face on the earth and her sorrow and groaning and wailing was redoubled. And she cried out and said: 'Woe unto thee, thou devil. Why dost thou attack us for no cause What hast thou to do with us What have we done to thee for thou pursuest us with craft Or why doth thy malice assail us Have we taken away thy glory and caused thee to be without honour Why dost thou harry us, thou enemy (and persecute us) to the death in wickedness and envy'$n$,'canon','zechariah',3,2,$n$And Yahuah (LORD) said unto Satan, Yahuah (LORD) rebuke thee, O Satan; even Yahuah (LORD) that hath chosen Jerusalem rebuke thee: is not this a brand plucked out of the fire?$n$,'free',$n$Zechariah 3:2 — *And Yahuah (LORD) said unto Satan, Yahuah (LORD) rebuke thee, O Satan; even Yahuah (LORD) that hath chosen Jerusalem rebuke thee: is not this a brand plucked out of the fire?* *And when she heard this, Eve understood that (it was) the devil (who) had persuaded her to go out of the river; and she fell on her face on the earth and her sorrow and groaning and wailing was redoubled. And she cried out and said: 'Woe unto thee, thou devil. Why dost thou attack us for no cause What hast thou to do with us What have we done to thee for thou pursuest us with craft Or why doth thy malice assail us Have we taken away thy glory and caused thee to be without honour Why dost thou harry us, thou enemy (and persecute us) to the death in wickedness and envy'* (Adam and Eve 54:1) Eve's rebuke is her own, and Zechariah has it come from Yahuah (the LORD): Yahuah (LORD) rebuke thee, O Satan. In the vision the rebuke is not the priest's but his, and the one rebuked is a brand plucked out of the fire.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',55,1,$n$And with a heavy sigh, the devil spake: 'O Adam! all my hostility, envy, and sorrow is for thee, since it is for thee that I have been expelled from my glory, which I possessed in the heavens in the midst of the angels and for thee was I cast out in the earth.' Adam answered, 'What dost thou tell me What have I done to thee or what is my fault against thee Seeing that thou hast received no harm or injury from us, why dost thou pursue us'$n$,'canon','ezekiel',28,16,$n$By the multitude of thy merchandise they have filled the midst of thee with violence, and thou hast sinned: therefore I will cast thee as profane out of the mountain of Elohim (God): and I will destroy thee, O covering cherub, from the midst of the stones of fire.$n$,'free',$n$Ezekiel 28:16 — *By the multitude of thy merchandise they have filled the midst of thee with violence, and thou hast sinned: therefore I will cast thee as profane out of the mountain of Elohim (God): and I will destroy thee, O covering cherub, from the midst of the stones of fire.* *And with a heavy sigh, the devil spake: 'O Adam! all my hostility, envy, and sorrow is for thee, since it is for thee that I have been expelled from my glory, which I possessed in the heavens in the midst of the angels and for thee was I cast out in the earth.' Adam answered, 'What dost thou tell me What have I done to thee or what is my fault against thee Seeing that thou hast received no harm or injury from us, why dost thou pursue us'* (Adam and Eve 55:1) The devil says he was expelled from his glory for Adam's sake. Ezekiel's word to the covering cherub gives another cause, in the voice of Elohim (God): thou hast sinned, therefore I will cast thee out of the mountain of Elohim (God). This book lays the blame on Adam, and the prophet lays it on the one who fell.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',55,1,$n$And with a heavy sigh, the devil spake: 'O Adam! all my hostility, envy, and sorrow is for thee, since it is for thee that I have been expelled from my glory, which I possessed in the heavens in the midst of the angels and for thee was I cast out in the earth.' Adam answered, 'What dost thou tell me What have I done to thee or what is my fault against thee Seeing that thou hast received no harm or injury from us, why dost thou pursue us'$n$,'canon','luke',10,18,$n$And he said unto them, I beheld Satan as lightning fall from heaven.$n$,'free',$n$Luke 10:18 — *And he said unto them, I beheld Satan as lightning fall from heaven.* *And with a heavy sigh, the devil spake: 'O Adam! all my hostility, envy, and sorrow is for thee, since it is for thee that I have been expelled from my glory, which I possessed in the heavens in the midst of the angels and for thee was I cast out in the earth.' Adam answered, 'What dost thou tell me What have I done to thee or what is my fault against thee Seeing that thou hast received no harm or injury from us, why dost thou pursue us'* (Adam and Eve 55:1) Yahusha (Jesus) says, I beheld Satan as lightning fall from heaven. The fall is real and it is his, seen by the One who was there, and the one who fell does not get to say whom he fell for.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',57,1,$n$And Michael went out and called all the angels saying: 'Worship the image of Elohim (God) as Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) hath commanded.' And Michael himself worshipped first; then he called me and said: 'Worship the image of Elohim (God) Yahuah (Lord).' And I answered, 'I have no (need) to worship Adam.' And since Michael kept urging me to worship, I said to him, 'Why dost thou urge me I will not worship an inferior and younger being (than I). I am his senior in the Creation, before he was made was I already made. It is his duty to worship me.'$n$,'canon','genesis',1,26,$n$And Elohim (God) said, Let us make man in our image, after our likeness: and let them have dominion over the fish of the sea, and over the fowl of the air, and over the cattle, and over all the earth, and over every creeping thing that creepeth upon the earth.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 1:26 — *And Elohim (God) said, Let us make man in our image, after our likeness: and let them have dominion over the fish of the sea, and over the fowl of the air, and over the cattle, and over all the earth, and over every creeping thing that creepeth upon the earth.* *And Michael went out and called all the angels saying: 'Worship the image of Elohim (God) as Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) hath commanded.' And Michael himself worshipped first; then he called me and said: 'Worship the image of Elohim (God) Yahuah (Lord).' And I answered, 'I have no (need) to worship Adam.' And since Michael kept urging me to worship, I said to him, 'Why dost thou urge me I will not worship an inferior and younger being (than I). I am his senior in the Creation, before he was made was I already made. It is his duty to worship me.'* (Adam and Eve 57:1) Michael calls the angels to worship the image of Elohim (God), and the devil answers that he is the elder and will not. Moses gives the image its source: Let us make man in our image, with dominion over all the earth. The man carries the image, and the dominion is his charge.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',57,1,$n$And Michael went out and called all the angels saying: 'Worship the image of Elohim (God) as Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) hath commanded.' And Michael himself worshipped first; then he called me and said: 'Worship the image of Elohim (God) Yahuah (Lord).' And I answered, 'I have no (need) to worship Adam.' And since Michael kept urging me to worship, I said to him, 'Why dost thou urge me I will not worship an inferior and younger being (than I). I am his senior in the Creation, before he was made was I already made. It is his duty to worship me.'$n$,'canon','revelation',22,9,$n$Then saith he unto me, See thou do it not: for I am thy fellowservant, and of thy brethren the prophets, and of them which keep the sayings of this book: worship Elohim (God).$n$,'free',$n$Revelation 22:9 — *Then saith he unto me, See thou do it not: for I am thy fellowservant, and of thy brethren the prophets, and of them which keep the sayings of this book: worship Elohim (God).* *And Michael went out and called all the angels saying: 'Worship the image of Elohim (God) as Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) hath commanded.' And Michael himself worshipped first; then he called me and said: 'Worship the image of Elohim (God) Yahuah (Lord).' And I answered, 'I have no (need) to worship Adam.' And since Michael kept urging me to worship, I said to him, 'Why dost thou urge me I will not worship an inferior and younger being (than I). I am his senior in the Creation, before he was made was I already made. It is his duty to worship me.'* (Adam and Eve 57:1) In the canon, worship goes to Elohim (God) alone. When John falls down before an angel, he is stopped: See thou do it not ... worship Elohim (God). The command to worship Adam as the image is this book's own; the canon keeps worship for Elohim (God) and lets the reader see how much honour the image is owed.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',60,1,$n$When Adam heard the devil say this, he cried out and wept and spake: 'O Yahuah (Lord) my Elohim (God), my life is in thy hands. Banish this Adversary far from me, who seeketh to destroy my soul, and give me his glory which he himself hath lost.' And at that moment, the devil vanished before him. But Adam endured in his penance, standing for forty days (on end) in the water of Jordan.$n$,'canon','psalms',31,15,$n$My times are in thy hand: deliver me from the hand of mine enemies, and from them that persecute me.$n$,'free',$n$Psalm 31:15 — *My times are in thy hand: deliver me from the hand of mine enemies, and from them that persecute me.* *When Adam heard the devil say this, he cried out and wept and spake: 'O Yahuah (Lord) my Elohim (God), my life is in thy hands. Banish this Adversary far from me, who seeketh to destroy my soul, and give me his glory which he himself hath lost.' And at that moment, the devil vanished before him. But Adam endured in his penance, standing for forty days (on end) in the water of Jordan.* (Adam and Eve 60:1) Adam cries, my life is in thy hands, and asks that the Adversary be banished. David's prayer has the same shape: My times are in thy hand: deliver me from the hand of mine enemies, and from them that persecute me.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',60,1,$n$When Adam heard the devil say this, he cried out and wept and spake: 'O Yahuah (Lord) my Elohim (God), my life is in thy hands. Banish this Adversary far from me, who seeketh to destroy my soul, and give me his glory which he himself hath lost.' And at that moment, the devil vanished before him. But Adam endured in his penance, standing for forty days (on end) in the water of Jordan.$n$,'canon','psalms',69,1,$n$Save me, O Elohim (God); for the waters are come in unto my soul.$n$,'free',$n$Psalm 69:1 — *Save me, O Elohim (God); for the waters are come in unto my soul.* *When Adam heard the devil say this, he cried out and wept and spake: 'O Yahuah (Lord) my Elohim (God), my life is in thy hands. Banish this Adversary far from me, who seeketh to destroy my soul, and give me his glory which he himself hath lost.' And at that moment, the devil vanished before him. But Adam endured in his penance, standing for forty days (on end) in the water of Jordan.* (Adam and Eve 60:1) Adam stands forty days in the Jordan, in water up to his neck. The psalm cries from the same place: Save me, O Elohim (God); for the waters are come in unto my soul.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',63,2,$n$And he went and found her in great distress. And Eve said: 'From the moment I saw thee, my lord, my grief-laden soul was refreshed. And now entreat Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) on my behalf to hearken unto thee and look upon me and free me from my awful pains.' And Adam entreated Yahuah (Lord) for Eve.$n$,'canon','genesis',3,16,$n$Unto the woman he said, I will greatly multiply thy sorrow and thy conception; in sorrow thou shalt bring forth children; and thy desire shall be to thy husband, and he shall rule over thee.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 3:16 — *Unto the woman he said, I will greatly multiply thy sorrow and thy conception; in sorrow thou shalt bring forth children; and thy desire shall be to thy husband, and he shall rule over thee.* *And he went and found her in great distress. And Eve said: 'From the moment I saw thee, my lord, my grief-laden soul was refreshed. And now entreat Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) on my behalf to hearken unto thee and look upon me and free me from my awful pains.' And Adam entreated Yahuah (Lord) for Eve.* (Adam and Eve 63:2) Eve is in great distress, and asks Adam to entreat Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) that her awful pains be freed. What she bears is what Genesis recorded: I will greatly multiply thy sorrow and thy conception; in sorrow thou shalt bring forth children.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',63,2,$n$And he went and found her in great distress. And Eve said: 'From the moment I saw thee, my lord, my grief-laden soul was refreshed. And now entreat Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) on my behalf to hearken unto thee and look upon me and free me from my awful pains.' And Adam entreated Yahuah (Lord) for Eve.$n$,'canon','john',16,21,$n$A woman when she is in travail hath sorrow, because her hour is come: but as soon as she is delivered of the child, she remembereth no more the anguish, for joy that a man is born into the world.$n$,'free',$n$John 16:21 — *A woman when she is in travail hath sorrow, because her hour is come: but as soon as she is delivered of the child, she remembereth no more the anguish, for joy that a man is born into the world.* *And he went and found her in great distress. And Eve said: 'From the moment I saw thee, my lord, my grief-laden soul was refreshed. And now entreat Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) on my behalf to hearken unto thee and look upon me and free me from my awful pains.' And Adam entreated Yahuah (Lord) for Eve.* (Adam and Eve 63:2) Yahusha (Jesus) speaks of the same anguish: A woman when she is in travail hath sorrow, because her hour is come. And then the joy: a man is born into the world. Eve's cry ends, in the next chapter, in a child.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',65,2,$n$For thereafter Eve conceived and bare a son, whose name was Abel; and Cain and Abel used to stay together.$n$,'canon','genesis',4,2,$n$And she again bare his brother Abel. And Abel was a keeper of sheep, but Cain was a tiller of the ground.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 4:2 — *And she again bare his brother Abel. And Abel was a keeper of sheep, but Cain was a tiller of the ground.* *For thereafter Eve conceived and bare a son, whose name was Abel; and Cain and Abel used to stay together.* (Adam and Eve 65:2) The witness says Cain and Abel used to stay together. Moses names them by their work: Abel was a keeper of sheep, but Cain was a tiller of the ground. These were the two lives that would later part them.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',65,3,$n$And Eve said to Adam: 'My lord, while I slept, I saw a vision, as it were the blood of our son Abel in the hand of Cain, who was gulping it down in his mouth. Therefore I have sorrow.'$n$,'canon','genesis',4,10,$n$And he said, What hast thou done? the voice of thy brother’s blood crieth unto me from the ground.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 4:10 — *And he said, What hast thou done? the voice of thy brother’s blood crieth unto me from the ground.* *And Eve said to Adam: 'My lord, while I slept, I saw a vision, as it were the blood of our son Abel in the hand of Cain, who was gulping it down in his mouth. Therefore I have sorrow.'* (Adam and Eve 65:3) Eve dreams the blood of Abel in the hand of Cain, gulped down in his mouth. Moses has the cry of that blood: the voice of thy brother's blood crieth unto me from the ground. The dream and the verdict describe one crime.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',65,4,$n$And Adam said, 'Alas if Cain slew Abel. Yet let us separate them from each other mutually, and let us make for each of them separate dwellings.'$n$,'canon','genesis',4,9,$n$And Yahuah (LORD) said unto Cain, Where is Abel thy brother? And he said, I know not: Am I my brother’s keeper?$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 4:9 — *And Yahuah (LORD) said unto Cain, Where is Abel thy brother? And he said, I know not: Am I my brother’s keeper?* *And Adam said, 'Alas if Cain slew Abel. Yet let us separate them from each other mutually, and let us make for each of them separate dwellings.'* (Adam and Eve 65:4) Adam plans to separate the brothers and give each a dwelling. In Genesis Cain answers Yahuah (the LORD): I know not: Am I my brother's keeper? Adam's wish to keep them apart is the father trying to be the keeper Cain would not be.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',70,1,$n$And when I heard these words of Elohim (God), I fell prone on the earth and worshipped Yahuah (Lord) and said, 'My Lord, All powerful and merciful Elohim (God), Holy and Righteous One, let not the name that is mindful of Thy majesty be blotted out, but convert my soul, for I die and my breath will go out of my mouth. Cast me not out from Thy presence, (me) whom Thou didst form of the clay of the earth. Do not banish from Thy favour him whom Thou didst nourish.'$n$,'canon','psalms',51,11,$n$Cast me not away from thy presence; and take not thy holy spirit from me.$n$,'free',$n$Psalm 51:11 — *Cast me not away from thy presence; and take not thy holy spirit from me.* *And when I heard these words of Elohim (God), I fell prone on the earth and worshipped Yahuah (Lord) and said, 'My Lord, All powerful and merciful Elohim (God), Holy and Righteous One, let not the name that is mindful of Thy majesty be blotted out, but convert my soul, for I die and my breath will go out of my mouth. Cast me not out from Thy presence, (me) whom Thou didst form of the clay of the earth. Do not banish from Thy favour him whom Thou didst nourish.'* (Adam and Eve 70:1) Adam falls prone and prays, Cast me not out from Thy presence. David prays it after his own fall: Cast me not away from thy presence; and take not thy holy spirit from me. It is the prayer of the one who has been cast out once.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',70,2,$n$And lo! a word concerning thee came upon me and Yahuah (Lord) said to me, 'Since thy days were fashioned, thou hast been created with a love of knowledge; therefore there shall not be taken from thy seed for ever the (right) to serve Me.'$n$,'canon','isaiah',66,22,$n$For as the new heavens and the new earth, which I will make, shall remain before me, saith Yahuah (LORD), so shall your seed and your name remain.$n$,'free',$n$Isaiah 66:22 — *For as the new heavens and the new earth, which I will make, shall remain before me, saith Yahuah (LORD), so shall your seed and your name remain.* *And lo! a word concerning thee came upon me and Yahuah (Lord) said to me, 'Since thy days were fashioned, thou hast been created with a love of knowledge; therefore there shall not be taken from thy seed for ever the (right) to serve Me.'* (Adam and Eve 70:2) Yahuah (the Lord) tells Adam that the right to serve him shall not be taken from his seed for ever. Isaiah has the same promise at the end of all things: as the new heavens and the new earth shall remain before me, so shall your seed and your name remain.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',71,2,$n$'Thou art the true Light gleaming above all light(s), the Living Life, infinite mighty Power. To Thee, the spiritual powers give honour and praise. Thou workest on the race of men the abundance of Thy mercy.'$n$,'canon','psalms',36,9,$n$For with thee is the fountain of life: in thy light shall we see light.$n$,'free',$n$Psalm 36:9 — *For with thee is the fountain of life: in thy light shall we see light.* *'Thou art the true Light gleaming above all light(s), the Living Life, infinite mighty Power. To Thee, the spiritual powers give honour and praise. Thou workest on the race of men the abundance of Thy mercy.'* (Adam and Eve 71:2) Adam says, Thou art the true Light gleaming above all light(s), the Living Life. The psalmist says it as a plain thing: with thee is the fountain of life; in thy light shall we see light.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',71,1,$n$And when I heard these words. I threw myself on the earth and adored Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) and said, 'Thou art the eternal and supreme Elohim (God); and all creatures give thee honour and praise.$n$,'canon','psalms',90,2,$n$Before the mountains were brought forth, or ever thou hadst formed the earth and the world, even from everlasting to everlasting, thou art Elohim (God).$n$,'free',$n$Psalm 90:2 — *Before the mountains were brought forth, or ever thou hadst formed the earth and the world, even from everlasting to everlasting, thou art Elohim (God).* *And when I heard these words. I threw myself on the earth and adored Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) and said, 'Thou art the eternal and supreme Elohim (God); and all creatures give thee honour and praise.* (Adam and Eve 71:1) Thou art the eternal and supreme Elohim (God), Adam says. The prayer of Moses is the same: from everlasting to everlasting, thou art Elohim (God). The One before the mountains is the One the first man worships.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',74,1,$n$Then said Seth his son: 'O (my) lord, perchance thou hast longed after the fruit of paradise, which thou wast wont to eat, and therefore thou liest in sadness Tell me and I will go to the nearest gates of paradise and put dust on my head and throw myself down on the earth before the gates of paradise and lament and make entreaty to Elohim (God) with loud lamentation; perchance he will hearken to me and send his angel to bring me the fruit, for which thou hast longed.'$n$,'canon','joshua',7,6,$n$And Joshua rent his clothes, and fell to the earth upon his face before the ark of Yahuah (LORD) until the eventide, he and the elders of Yashar'el (Israel), and put dust upon their heads.$n$,'free',$n$Joshua 7:6 — *And Joshua rent his clothes, and fell to the earth upon his face before the ark of Yahuah (LORD) until the eventide, he and the elders of Yashar'el (Israel), and put dust upon their heads.* *Then said Seth his son: 'O (my) lord, perchance thou hast longed after the fruit of paradise, which thou wast wont to eat, and therefore thou liest in sadness Tell me and I will go to the nearest gates of paradise and put dust on my head and throw myself down on the earth before the gates of paradise and lament and make entreaty to Elohim (God) with loud lamentation; perchance he will hearken to me and send his angel to bring me the fruit, for which thou hast longed.'* (Adam and Eve 74:1) Seth offers to put dust on his head and throw himself down before the gates of paradise. That is what Joshua and the elders of Yashar'el (Israel) did before the ark: they rent their clothes, fell to the earth on their faces, and put dust upon their heads.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',74,2,$n$Adam answered and said: 'No, my son, I do not long (for this), but I feel weakness and great pain in my body.' Seth answered, 'What is pain, my lord father I am ignorant; but hide it not from us, but tell us (about it).'$n$,'canon','psalms',38,3,$n$There is no soundness in my flesh because of thine anger; neither is there any rest in my bones because of my sin.$n$,'free',$n$Psalm 38:3 — *There is no soundness in my flesh because of thine anger; neither is there any rest in my bones because of my sin.* *Adam answered and said: 'No, my son, I do not long (for this), but I feel weakness and great pain in my body.' Seth answered, 'What is pain, my lord father I am ignorant; but hide it not from us, but tell us (about it).'* (Adam and Eve 74:2) Adam says he has weakness and great pain in his body, and Seth does not know what pain is. David names it and says where it came from: no soundness in my flesh because of thine anger, no rest in my bones because of my sin.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',75,1,$n$And Adam answered and said: 'Hear me, my sons. When Elohim (God) made us, me and your mother, and placed us in paradise and gave us every tree bearing fruit to eat, he laid a prohibition on us concerning the tree of knowledge of good and evil, which is in the midst of paradise; (saying) 'Do not eat of it.' But Elohim (God) gave a part of paradise to me and (a part) to your mother: the trees of the eastern part and the north, which is over against Aquilo he gave to me, and to your mother he gave the part of the south and the western part.$n$,'canon','genesis',2,17,$n$But of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, thou shalt not eat of it: for in the day that thou eatest thereof thou shalt surely die.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 2:17 — *But of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, thou shalt not eat of it: for in the day that thou eatest thereof thou shalt surely die.* *And Adam answered and said: 'Hear me, my sons. When Elohim (God) made us, me and your mother, and placed us in paradise and gave us every tree bearing fruit to eat, he laid a prohibition on us concerning the tree of knowledge of good and evil, which is in the midst of paradise; (saying) 'Do not eat of it.' But Elohim (God) gave a part of paradise to me and (a part) to your mother: the trees of the eastern part and the north, which is over against Aquilo he gave to me, and to your mother he gave the part of the south and the western part.* (Adam and Eve 75:1) Adam tells his sons that Elohim (God) laid a prohibition on them concerning the tree of knowledge. Moses gives it with its consequence: in the day that thou eatest thereof thou shalt surely die. The command carried its warning with it.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',75,1,$n$And Adam answered and said: 'Hear me, my sons. When Elohim (God) made us, me and your mother, and placed us in paradise and gave us every tree bearing fruit to eat, he laid a prohibition on us concerning the tree of knowledge of good and evil, which is in the midst of paradise; (saying) 'Do not eat of it.' But Elohim (God) gave a part of paradise to me and (a part) to your mother: the trees of the eastern part and the north, which is over against Aquilo he gave to me, and to your mother he gave the part of the south and the western part.$n$,'canon','genesis',2,9,$n$And out of the ground made Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) to grow every tree that is pleasant to the sight, and good for food; the tree of life also in the midst of the garden, and the tree of knowledge of good and evil.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 2:9 — *And out of the ground made Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) to grow every tree that is pleasant to the sight, and good for food; the tree of life also in the midst of the garden, and the tree of knowledge of good and evil.* *And Adam answered and said: 'Hear me, my sons. When Elohim (God) made us, me and your mother, and placed us in paradise and gave us every tree bearing fruit to eat, he laid a prohibition on us concerning the tree of knowledge of good and evil, which is in the midst of paradise; (saying) 'Do not eat of it.' But Elohim (God) gave a part of paradise to me and (a part) to your mother: the trees of the eastern part and the north, which is over against Aquilo he gave to me, and to your mother he gave the part of the south and the western part.* (Adam and Eve 75:1) Adam says the tree of knowledge was in the midst of paradise. Moses places both trees there, the tree of life also in the midst of the garden, and the tree of knowledge of good and evil. The division of the garden into portions for Adam and for Eve is this book's own, and Genesis does not say it.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',77,1,$n$And immediately, Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) was wrath with us, and Yahuah (Lord) said to me: 'In that thou hast left behind my commandment and hast not kept my word, which I confirmed to thee; behold, I will bring upon thy body, seventy blows; with divers griefs, shalt thou be tormented, beginning at thy head and thine eyes and thine ears down to thy nails on thy toes, and in every separate limb. These hath Elohim (God) appointed for chastisement. All these things hath Yahuah (Lord) sent to me and to all our race.'$n$,'canon','deuteronomy',28,59,$n$Then Yahuah (LORD) will make thy plagues wonderful, and the plagues of thy seed, even great plagues, and of long continuance, and sore sicknesses, and of long continuance.$n$,'free',$n$Deuteronomy 28:59 — *Then Yahuah (LORD) will make thy plagues wonderful, and the plagues of thy seed, even great plagues, and of long continuance, and sore sicknesses, and of long continuance.* *And immediately, Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) was wrath with us, and Yahuah (Lord) said to me: 'In that thou hast left behind my commandment and hast not kept my word, which I confirmed to thee; behold, I will bring upon thy body, seventy blows; with divers griefs, shalt thou be tormented, beginning at thy head and thine eyes and thine ears down to thy nails on thy toes, and in every separate limb. These hath Elohim (God) appointed for chastisement. All these things hath Yahuah (Lord) sent to me and to all our race.'* (Adam and Eve 77:1) Seventy blows are the number of this book. In Moses' covenant the same kind of judgment for leaving the commandment is plagues of thy seed and sore sicknesses. The curses of Deuteronomy 28 are the judgment for covenant-breaking, and they fall on the seed.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',77,1,$n$And immediately, Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) was wrath with us, and Yahuah (Lord) said to me: 'In that thou hast left behind my commandment and hast not kept my word, which I confirmed to thee; behold, I will bring upon thy body, seventy blows; with divers griefs, shalt thou be tormented, beginning at thy head and thine eyes and thine ears down to thy nails on thy toes, and in every separate limb. These hath Elohim (God) appointed for chastisement. All these things hath Yahuah (Lord) sent to me and to all our race.'$n$,'canon','romans',5,12,$n$Wherefore, as by one man sin entered into the world, and death by sin; and so death passed upon all men, for that all have sinned:$n$,'free',$n$Romans 5:12 — *Wherefore, as by one man sin entered into the world, and death by sin; and so death passed upon all men, for that all have sinned:* *And immediately, Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) was wrath with us, and Yahuah (Lord) said to me: 'In that thou hast left behind my commandment and hast not kept my word, which I confirmed to thee; behold, I will bring upon thy body, seventy blows; with divers griefs, shalt thou be tormented, beginning at thy head and thine eyes and thine ears down to thy nails on thy toes, and in every separate limb. These hath Elohim (God) appointed for chastisement. All these things hath Yahuah (Lord) sent to me and to all our race.'* (Adam and Eve 77:1) All these things hath Yahuah (the Lord) sent to me and to all our race, Adam says. Paul states the reach in one sentence: as by one man sin entered into the world, and death by sin; and so death passed upon all men.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',78,1,$n$Thus spake Adam to his sons, and he was seized with violent pains, and he cried out with a loud voice, 'What shall I do I am in distress. So cruel are the pains with which I am beset.' And when Eve had seen him weeping, she also began to weep herself, and said: 'O Yahuah (Lord) my Elohim (God), hand over to me his pain, for it is I who sinned.'$n$,'canon','2-samuel',24,17,$n$And David spake unto Yahuah (LORD) when he saw the angel that smote the people, and said, Lo, I have sinned, and I have done wickedly: but these sheep, what have they done? let thine hand, I pray thee, be against me, and against my father’s house.$n$,'free',$n$2 Samuel 24:17 — *And David spake unto Yahuah (LORD) when he saw the angel that smote the people, and said, Lo, I have sinned, and I have done wickedly: but these sheep, what have they done? let thine hand, I pray thee, be against me, and against my father’s house.* *Thus spake Adam to his sons, and he was seized with violent pains, and he cried out with a loud voice, 'What shall I do I am in distress. So cruel are the pains with which I am beset.' And when Eve had seen him weeping, she also began to weep herself, and said: 'O Yahuah (Lord) my Elohim (God), hand over to me his pain, for it is I who sinned.'* (Adam and Eve 78:1) Eve asks Yahuah (the Lord) to hand over to her his pain, for it is she who sinned. David prays at the plague in the same spirit: Lo, I have sinned, and I have done wickedly, let thine hand be against me. A sinner who asks to stand where the stroke is falling.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',78,2,$n$And Eve said to Adam: 'My lord, give me a part of thy pains, for this hath come to thee from fault of mine.'$n$,'canon','galatians',6,2,$n$Bear ye one another’s burdens, and so fulfil the law of Messiah (Christ).$n$,'free',$n$Galatians 6:2 — *Bear ye one another’s burdens, and so fulfil the law of Messiah (Christ).* *And Eve said to Adam: 'My lord, give me a part of thy pains, for this hath come to thee from fault of mine.'* (Adam and Eve 78:2) Eve says, give me a part of thy pains, for this hath come to thee from fault of mine. Paul writes: Bear ye one another's burdens, and so fulfil the law of Messiah (Christ). A wife taking what she can of her husband's load is that command at its smallest.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',81,1,$n$The beast answered in the language of men: 'Is it not against you, Eve, that our malice (is directed) Are not ye the objects of our rage Tell me, Eve, how was thy mouth opened to eat of the fruit But now if I shall begin to reprove thee thou canst not bear it.'$n$,'canon','numbers',22,28,$n$And Yahuah (LORD) opened the mouth of the ass, and she said unto Balaam, What have I done unto thee, that thou hast smitten me these three times?$n$,'free',$n$Numbers 22:28 — *And Yahuah (LORD) opened the mouth of the ass, and she said unto Balaam, What have I done unto thee, that thou hast smitten me these three times?* *The beast answered in the language of men: 'Is it not against you, Eve, that our malice (is directed) Are not ye the objects of our rage Tell me, Eve, how was thy mouth opened to eat of the fruit But now if I shall begin to reprove thee thou canst not bear it.'* (Adam and Eve 81:1) The beast answers in the language of men. Moses records another beast that spoke: Yahuah (the LORD) opened the mouth of the ass, and she said unto Balaam, What have I done unto thee? A beast speaks when Yahuah (the LORD) gives it voice.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',81,1,$n$The beast answered in the language of men: 'Is it not against you, Eve, that our malice (is directed) Are not ye the objects of our rage Tell me, Eve, how was thy mouth opened to eat of the fruit But now if I shall begin to reprove thee thou canst not bear it.'$n$,'canon','genesis',9,2,$n$And the fear of you and the dread of you shall be upon every beast of the earth, and upon every fowl of the air, upon all that moveth upon the earth, and upon all the fishes of the sea; into your hand are they delivered.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 9:2 — *And the fear of you and the dread of you shall be upon every beast of the earth, and upon every fowl of the air, upon all that moveth upon the earth, and upon all the fishes of the sea; into your hand are they delivered.* *The beast answered in the language of men: 'Is it not against you, Eve, that our malice (is directed) Are not ye the objects of our rage Tell me, Eve, how was thy mouth opened to eat of the fruit But now if I shall begin to reprove thee thou canst not bear it.'* (Adam and Eve 81:1) The beast says, Is it not against you, Eve, that our malice is directed? After the flood Moses records that the fear of man and the dread of him came upon every beast. The enmity between beast and man is old, and this book gives it a voice.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',83,1,$n$But Seth and his mother walked to the regions of paradise for the oil of mercy to anoint the sick Adam: and they arrived at the gates of paradise, (and) they took dust from the earth and placed it on their heads, and bowed themselves with their faces to the earth and began to lament and make loud moaning, imploring Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) to pity Adam in his pains and to send His angel to give them the oil from the 'tree of His mercy'.$n$,'canon','james',5,14,$n$Is any sick among you? let him call for the elders of the church; and let them pray over him, anointing him with oil in the name of Yahuah (Lord):$n$,'free',$n$James 5:14 — *Is any sick among you? let him call for the elders of the church; and let them pray over him, anointing him with oil in the name of Yahuah (Lord):* *But Seth and his mother walked to the regions of paradise for the oil of mercy to anoint the sick Adam: and they arrived at the gates of paradise, (and) they took dust from the earth and placed it on their heads, and bowed themselves with their faces to the earth and began to lament and make loud moaning, imploring Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) to pity Adam in his pains and to send His angel to give them the oil from the 'tree of His mercy'.* (Adam and Eve 83:1) Seth and Eve go for the oil to anoint sick Adam. James writes of the sick in the assembly calling for the elders, who pray over him, anointing him with oil in the name of Yahuah (Lord). The oil here is gathered from a tree of mercy, and in James it is given with prayer.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',83,1,$n$But Seth and his mother walked to the regions of paradise for the oil of mercy to anoint the sick Adam: and they arrived at the gates of paradise, (and) they took dust from the earth and placed it on their heads, and bowed themselves with their faces to the earth and began to lament and make loud moaning, imploring Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) to pity Adam in his pains and to send His angel to give them the oil from the 'tree of His mercy'.$n$,'canon','psalms',103,3,$n$Who forgiveth all thine iniquities; who healeth all thy diseases;$n$,'free',$n$Psalm 103:3 — *Who forgiveth all thine iniquities; who healeth all thy diseases;* *But Seth and his mother walked to the regions of paradise for the oil of mercy to anoint the sick Adam: and they arrived at the gates of paradise, (and) they took dust from the earth and placed it on their heads, and bowed themselves with their faces to the earth and began to lament and make loud moaning, imploring Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) to pity Adam in his pains and to send His angel to give them the oil from the 'tree of His mercy'.* (Adam and Eve 83:1) They kneel with dust on their heads, imploring Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) to pity Adam in his pains. The psalm names who answers: who forgiveth all thine iniquities; who healeth all thy diseases.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',84,1,$n$But when they had been praying and imploring for many hours, behold, the angel Michael appeared to them and said: 'I have been sent to you from Yahuah (Lord) -I am set by Elohim (God) over the bodies of men- I tell thee, Seth, (thou) man of Elohim (God), weep not nor pray and entreat on account of the oil of the tree of mercy to anoint thy father Adam for the pains of his body.$n$,'canon','revelation',22,2,$n$In the midst of the street of it, and on either side of the river, was there the tree of life, which bare twelve manner of fruits, and yielded her fruit every month: and the leaves of the tree were for the healing of the nations.$n$,'free',$n$Revelation 22:2 — *In the midst of the street of it, and on either side of the river, was there the tree of life, which bare twelve manner of fruits, and yielded her fruit every month: and the leaves of the tree were for the healing of the nations.* *But when they had been praying and imploring for many hours, behold, the angel Michael appeared to them and said: 'I have been sent to you from Yahuah (Lord) -I am set by Elohim (God) over the bodies of men- I tell thee, Seth, (thou) man of Elohim (God), weep not nor pray and entreat on account of the oil of the tree of mercy to anoint thy father Adam for the pains of his body.* (Adam and Eve 84:1) Michael tells Seth not to weep for the oil of the tree of mercy. The canon sets the tree at the end, the tree of life by the river, whose leaves are for the healing of the nations. What cannot be fetched now is given in its time.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',84,1,$n$But when they had been praying and imploring for many hours, behold, the angel Michael appeared to them and said: 'I have been sent to you from Yahuah (Lord) -I am set by Elohim (God) over the bodies of men- I tell thee, Seth, (thou) man of Elohim (God), weep not nor pray and entreat on account of the oil of the tree of mercy to anoint thy father Adam for the pains of his body.$n$,'canon','habakkuk',2,3,$n$For the vision is yet for an appointed time, but at the end it shall speak, and not lie: though it tarry, wait for it; because it will surely come, it will not tarry.$n$,'free',$n$Habakkuk 2:3 — *For the vision is yet for an appointed time, but at the end it shall speak, and not lie: though it tarry, wait for it; because it will surely come, it will not tarry.* *But when they had been praying and imploring for many hours, behold, the angel Michael appeared to them and said: 'I have been sent to you from Yahuah (Lord) -I am set by Elohim (God) over the bodies of men- I tell thee, Seth, (thou) man of Elohim (God), weep not nor pray and entreat on account of the oil of the tree of mercy to anoint thy father Adam for the pains of his body.* (Adam and Eve 84:1) Not now, but later, is Michael's answer. Habakkuk has the same word: the vision is yet for an appointed time, but at the end it shall speak, and not lie; though it tarry, wait for it.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',86,1,$n$'But do thou, Seth, go to thy father Adam, since the time of his life is fulfilled. Six days hence, his soul shall go off his body and when it shall have gone out, thou shalt see great marvels in the heaven and in the earth and the luminaries of heaven. With these words, straightway Michael departed from Seth.$n$,'canon','genesis',5,5,$n$And all the days that Adam lived were nine hundred and thirty years: and he died.$n$,'free',$n$Genesis 5:5 — *And all the days that Adam lived were nine hundred and thirty years: and he died.* *'But do thou, Seth, go to thy father Adam, since the time of his life is fulfilled. Six days hence, his soul shall go off his body and when it shall have gone out, thou shalt see great marvels in the heaven and in the earth and the luminaries of heaven. With these words, straightway Michael departed from Seth.* (Adam and Eve 86:1) Michael says the time of Adam's life is fulfilled, and that in six days his soul shall go off his body. Moses gives the total: all the days that Adam lived were nine hundred and thirty years: and he died.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',86,2,$n$And Eve and Seth returned bearing with them herbs of fragrance, i.e. nard and crocus and calamus and cinnamon.$n$,'canon','song-of-solomon',4,14,$n$Spikenard and saffron; calamus and cinnamon, with all trees of frankincense; myrrh and aloes, with all the chief spices:$n$,'free',$n$Song of Solomon 4:14 — *Spikenard and saffron; calamus and cinnamon, with all trees of frankincense; myrrh and aloes, with all the chief spices:* *And Eve and Seth returned bearing with them herbs of fragrance, i.e. nard and crocus and calamus and cinnamon.* (Adam and Eve 86:2) Eve and Seth return with nard, crocus, calamus, and cinnamon. The four are named together in the Song: Spikenard and saffron; calamus and cinnamon. The herbs are the ordinary spices of a garden, not the oil of paradise.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',87,1,$n$And when Seth and his mother had reached Adam, they told him, how the beast [the serpent] bit Seth. And Adam said to Eve: 'What hast thou done A great plague hast thou brought upon us, transgression and sin for all our generations: and this which thou hast done, tell thy children after my death, [for those who arise from us shall toil and fail but they shall be wanting and curse us (and) say, All evils have our parents brought upon us, who were at the beginning].' When Eve heard these words, she began to weep and moan.$n$,'canon','1-corinthians',15,22,$n$For as in Adam all die, even so in Messiah (Christ) shall all be made alive.$n$,'free',$n$1 Corinthians 15:22 — *For as in Adam all die, even so in Messiah (Christ) shall all be made alive.* *And when Seth and his mother had reached Adam, they told him, how the beast [the serpent] bit Seth. And Adam said to Eve: 'What hast thou done A great plague hast thou brought upon us, transgression and sin for all our generations: and this which thou hast done, tell thy children after my death, [for those who arise from us shall toil and fail but they shall be wanting and curse us (and) say, All evils have our parents brought upon us, who were at the beginning].' When Eve heard these words, she began to weep and moan.* (Adam and Eve 87:1) Adam tells Eve she has brought a great plague, transgression and sin for all generations. Paul states the reach in the same breath he states the answer: For as in Adam all die, even so in Messiah (Christ) shall all be made alive.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',87,1,$n$And when Seth and his mother had reached Adam, they told him, how the beast [the serpent] bit Seth. And Adam said to Eve: 'What hast thou done A great plague hast thou brought upon us, transgression and sin for all our generations: and this which thou hast done, tell thy children after my death, [for those who arise from us shall toil and fail but they shall be wanting and curse us (and) say, All evils have our parents brought upon us, who were at the beginning].' When Eve heard these words, she began to weep and moan.$n$,'canon','ezekiel',18,20,$n$The soul that sinneth, it shall die. The son shall not bear the iniquity of the father, neither shall the father bear the iniquity of the son: the righteousness of the righteous shall be upon him, and the wickedness of the wicked shall be upon him.$n$,'free',$n$Ezekiel 18:20 — *The soul that sinneth, it shall die. The son shall not bear the iniquity of the father, neither shall the father bear the iniquity of the son: the righteousness of the righteous shall be upon him, and the wickedness of the wicked shall be upon him.* *And when Seth and his mother had reached Adam, they told him, how the beast [the serpent] bit Seth. And Adam said to Eve: 'What hast thou done A great plague hast thou brought upon us, transgression and sin for all our generations: and this which thou hast done, tell thy children after my death, [for those who arise from us shall toil and fail but they shall be wanting and curse us (and) say, All evils have our parents brought upon us, who were at the beginning].' When Eve heard these words, she began to weep and moan.* (Adam and Eve 87:1) Adam expects his children to curse their parents and say All evils have our parents brought upon us. Ezekiel answers the complaint beforehand: The son shall not bear the iniquity of the father. Each answers for his own way, and the way home is open to each.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',90,1,$n$'Blessed art thou, O Yahuah (Lord), for thou hast had pity on Thy creature.'$n$,'canon','psalms',145,9,$n$Yahuah (LORD) is good to all: and his tender mercies are over all his works.$n$,'free',$n$Psalm 145:9 — *Yahuah (LORD) is good to all: and his tender mercies are over all his works.* *'Blessed art thou, O Yahuah (Lord), for thou hast had pity on Thy creature.'* (Adam and Eve 90:1) Blessed art thou, O Yahuah (Lord), for thou hast had pity on Thy creature, Adam says at the end. The psalmist gives the ground of the blessing: Yahuah (the LORD) is good to all: and his tender mercies are over all his works.$n$),
('pseudepigrapha','adam-eve',90,1,$n$'Blessed art thou, O Yahuah (Lord), for thou hast had pity on Thy creature.'$n$,'canon','luke',1,68,$n$Blessed be Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) of Yashar'el (Israel); for he hath visited and redeemed his people,$n$,'free',$n$Luke 1:68 — *Blessed be Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) of Yashar'el (Israel); for he hath visited and redeemed his people,* *'Blessed art thou, O Yahuah (Lord), for thou hast had pity on Thy creature.'* (Adam and Eve 90:1) The blessing is Zechariah's too, at the dawn of the gospel: Blessed be Yahuah Elohim (the Lord God) of Yashar'el (Israel); for he hath visited and redeemed his people. The old word of thanks for pity is spoken again when the pity arrives.$n$);
INSERT INTO _s495_adam_eve_t VALUES
('adam-eve-6-seth-offers-to-fetch-the-fruit-of-paradise',$n$Seth offers to fetch the fruit of paradise$n$,$n$Adam lies sick, and Seth offers to go for the fruit: *I will go and bring thee fruit from paradise* (Adam and Eve 6:1). It is a son's love, and it meets what Moses wrote of that tree: *And Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) said, Behold, the man is become as one of us, to know good and evil: and now, lest he put forth his hand, and take also of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever:* (Genesis 3:22) Then Yahuah Elohim (the LORD God) set a guard where the man could not pass: *So he drove out the man; and he placed at the east of the garden of Eden Cherubims, and a flaming sword which turned every way, to keep the way of the tree of life.* (Genesis 3:24) No son of Adam walks back in on his own errand.
INSERT INTO _s495_adam_eve_m VALUES
('adam-eve-3-abel-murdered-by-cain',2,1,'canon','genesis',4,11,6,$n$Genesis 4:11 — *which hath opened her mouth to receive thy brother’s blood from thy hand.* The earth that opens her mouth for Abel's blood is the dream's mouth made plain.$n$),
('adam-eve-3-abel-murdered-by-cain',2,1,'canon','1-peter',5,8,7,$n$1 Peter 5:8 — *your adversary the devil, as a roaring lion, walketh about, seeking whom he may devour.* Adam's dread of the adversary is the apostle's warning given a household.$n$),
('adam-eve-6-seth-offers-to-fetch-the-fruit-of-paradise',6,1,'canon','genesis',3,22,1,$n$Genesis 3:22 — *lest he put forth his hand, and take also of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever.* The fruit Seth offers to fetch is the tree Genesis says was guarded from the man.$n$),
('adam-eve-6-seth-offers-to-fetch-the-fruit-of-paradise',6,1,'canon','revelation',22,14,2,$n$Revelation 22:14 — *that they may have right to the tree of life.* The tree Seth would fetch for is given at the end to those who do his commandments.$n$),
('adam-eve-11-adam-reproves-eve-for-the-tree',11,1,'canon','genesis',3,17,1,$n$Genesis 3:17 — *Because thou hast hearkened unto the voice of thy wife, and hast eaten of the tree, of which I commanded thee.* In Genesis the sentence falls on the man who ate, not only on the woman who gave.$n$),
('adam-eve-11-adam-reproves-eve-for-the-tree',11,1,'canon','genesis',3,12,2,$n$Genesis 3:12 — *The woman whom thou gavest to be with me, she gave me of the tree, and I did eat.* The blame passed across the household in the garden is still being passed here.$n$),
('adam-eve-15-guarding-paradise-in-the-allotted-portion',15,1,'canon','genesis',2,15,1,$n$Genesis 2:15 — *to dress it and to keep it.* The guarding of paradise in Eve's account is the keeping Genesis gives to the man.$n$),
('adam-eve-15-guarding-paradise-in-the-allotted-portion',15,1,'canon','genesis',2,19,2,$n$Genesis 2:19 — *brought them unto Adam to see what he would call them.* Genesis puts the creatures before Adam; the division in Eve's account is not in Moses.$n$),
('adam-eve-16-the-devil-counsels-the-serpent',16,2,'canon','genesis',3,1,1,$n$Genesis 3:1 — *Yea, hath Elohim (God) said, Ye shall not eat of every tree of the garden?.* The question the serpent put to the woman is the devil's plan spoken.$n$),
('adam-eve-16-the-devil-counsels-the-serpent',16,1,'canon','revelation',12,9,2,$n$Revelation 12:9 — *that old serpent, called the Devil, and Satan, which deceiveth the whole world.* The devil and the serpent in Eve's account are named together in Revelation.$n$),
('adam-eve-27-thou-art-just-and-thy-judgement-is-righteous',27,1,'canon','psalms',119,137,1,$n$Psalm 119:137 — *Righteous art thou, O Yahuah (LORD), and upright are thy judgments.* The angels' worship of a just judgement is the psalmist's confession.$n$),
('adam-eve-27-thou-art-just-and-thy-judgement-is-righteous',27,1,'canon','genesis',3,24,2,$n$Genesis 3:24 — *So he drove out the man.* The expulsion the angels carry out is the driving out Genesis records.$n$),
('adam-eve-30-guard-yourselves-from-transgressing-against-the-good',30,1,'canon','deuteronomy',4,9,1,$n$Deuteronomy 4:9 — *but teach them thy sons, and thy sons’ sons.* Eve's charge to her children's children is Moses' charge to Yashar'el (Israel).$n$),
('adam-eve-30-guard-yourselves-from-transgressing-against-the-good',30,1,'canon','genesis',3,13,2,$n$Genesis 3:13 — *The serpent beguiled me, and I did eat.* Eve's whole account to her children is already in her one answer in the garden.$n$),
('adam-eve-36-sun-and-moon-cannot-shine',34,1,'canon','psalms',148,3,4,$n$Psalm 148:3 — *Praise ye him, sun and moon: praise him, all ye stars of light.* The wonders before the throne are the sun and moon the psalm summons to praise.$n$),
('adam-eve-36-sun-and-moon-cannot-shine',34,1,'canon','genesis',1,16,5,$n$Genesis 1:16 — *Elohim (God) made two great lights.* The lights Eve sees are the two great lights made on the fourth day.$n$),
('adam-eve-37-angels-pray-for-adams-soul',35,1,'canon','genesis',1,27,4,$n$Genesis 1:27 — *So Elohim (God) created man in his own image.* The angels' plea that he is Thine image is the creation word in Genesis.$n$),
('adam-eve-37-angels-pray-for-adams-soul',35,1,'canon','ezekiel',1,1,5,$n$Ezekiel 1:1 — *the heavens were opened, and I saw visions of Elohim (God).* The heavens opened over Eve and Seth are the heavens opened over Ezekiel.$n$),
('adam-eve-38-the-lord-of-hosts-comes-to-the-body-of-adam',38,1,'canon','psalms',18,10,1,$n$Psalm 18:10 — *he rode upon a cherub, and did fly.* The cherubim on the winds are the cherub and wings of the wind in the psalm.$n$),
('adam-eve-38-the-lord-of-hosts-comes-to-the-body-of-adam',38,1,'canon','genesis',4,25,2,$n$Genesis 4:25 — *Elohim (God), said she, hath appointed me another seed instead of Abel.* Seth born according to the appointment is Eve's word at his birth in Genesis.$n$),
('adam-eve-44-a-booth-and-seven-days-of-mourning',44,1,'canon','genesis',3,23,1,$n$Genesis 3:23 — *sent him forth from the garden of Eden, to till the ground from whence he was taken.* The sending forth in Genesis is where the booth and the mourning begin.$n$),
('adam-eve-44-a-booth-and-seven-days-of-mourning',44,1,'canon','joel',2,12,2,$n$Joel 2:12 — *turn ye even to me with all your heart, and with fasting, and with weeping, and with mourning.* The grief of the booth is the weeping Joel names as the road back.$n$),
('adam-eve-45-hunger-and-the-hope-of-being-recalled',45,1,'canon','genesis',3,19,1,$n$Genesis 3:19 — *In the sweat of thy face shalt thou eat bread.* Eve's hunger is the first meal of the sentence Genesis records.$n$),
('adam-eve-45-hunger-and-the-hope-of-being-recalled',45,1,'canon','psalms',103,13,2,$n$Psalm 103:13 — *Like as a father pitieth his children, so Yahuah (LORD) pitieth them that fear him.* Eve's hope of pity is the fatherly pity the psalm promises.$n$),
('adam-eve-46-wilt-thou-slay-me',46,2,'canon','genesis',2,23,1,$n$Genesis 2:23 — *This is now bone of my bones, and flesh of my flesh.* Adam's refusal to touch his own flesh is the first word he spoke of Eve.$n$),
('adam-eve-46-wilt-thou-slay-me',46,2,'canon','genesis',2,24,2,$n$Genesis 2:24 — *and they shall be one flesh.* The two who must live on together are the one flesh Genesis names.$n$),
('adam-eve-48-what-is-penitence',48,1,'canon','ecclesiastes',5,4,1,$n$Ecclesiastes 5:4 — *When thou vowest a vow unto Elohim (God), defer not to pay it.* Eve's dread of unpaid promises is the Preacher's warning on vows.$n$),
('adam-eve-48-what-is-penitence',48,1,'canon','psalms',51,17,2,$n$Psalm 51:17 — *a broken and a contrite heart, O Elohim (God), thou wilt not despise.* The question of how much penitence is answered in the heart, not the labour.$n$),
('adam-eve-49-fast-in-the-river-unclean-lips',50,1,'canon','genesis',2,14,5,$n$Genesis 2:14 — *the name of the third river is Hiddekel.* The river Eve stands in is one of those that came out of Eden.$n$),
('adam-eve-49-fast-in-the-river-unclean-lips',50,1,'canon','matthew',3,6,6,$n$Matthew 3:6 — *And were baptized of him in Jordan, confessing their sins.* The Jordan where Adam stands is the river where the people came to confess.$n$),
('adam-eve-53-the-devil-leads-eve-out-of-the-water',53,1,'canon','2-corinthians',11,14,1,$n$2 Corinthians 11:14 — *Satan himself is transformed into an angel of light.* The devil's kindness to Eve is the disguise Paul names.$n$),
('adam-eve-53-the-devil-leads-eve-out-of-the-water',53,2,'canon','2-timothy',2,26,2,$n$2 Timothy 2:26 — *recover themselves out of the snare of the devil.* Adam's word for being ensnared is Paul's word for the devil's snare.$n$),
('adam-eve-54-woe-unto-thee-devil',54,1,'canon','james',4,7,1,$n$James 4:7 — *Resist the devil, and he will flee from you.* Eve's cry against the devil is the beginning of the resisting James describes.$n$),
('adam-eve-54-woe-unto-thee-devil',54,1,'canon','zechariah',3,2,2,$n$Zechariah 3:2 — *Yahuah (LORD) rebuke thee, O Satan.* The rebuke Eve gives is, in the prophet's vision, spoken by Yahuah (the LORD).$n$),
('adam-eve-55-expelled-from-my-glory',55,1,'canon','ezekiel',28,16,1,$n$Ezekiel 28:16 — *thou hast sinned: therefore I will cast thee as profane out of the mountain of Elohim (God).* The casting out is, in the prophet, the answer to the cherub's own sin.$n$),
('adam-eve-55-expelled-from-my-glory',55,1,'canon','luke',10,18,2,$n$Luke 10:18 — *I beheld Satan as lightning fall from heaven.* The glory lost in the heavens is the fall Yahusha (Jesus) saw.$n$),
('adam-eve-56-the-image-of-elohim-the-breath-of-life',57,1,'canon','genesis',1,26,6,$n$Genesis 1:26 — *Let us make man in our image, after our likeness.* The image Michael tells the angels to honour is the image of Genesis 1.$n$),
('adam-eve-56-the-image-of-elohim-the-breath-of-life',57,1,'canon','revelation',22,9,7,$n$Revelation 22:9 — *See thou do it not: for I am thy fellowservant … worship Elohim (God).* The canon keeps worship for Elohim (God) alone, even before an angel.$n$),
('adam-eve-60-my-life-is-in-thy-hands',60,1,'canon','psalms',31,15,1,$n$Psalm 31:15 — *My times are in thy hand: deliver me from the hand of mine enemies.* Adam's prayer against the Adversary is David's prayer against his persecutors.$n$),
('adam-eve-60-my-life-is-in-thy-hands',60,1,'canon','psalms',69,1,2,$n$Psalm 69:1 — *Save me, O Elohim (God); for the waters are come in unto my soul.* The man standing in the Jordan prays what the psalmist prays in deep water.$n$),
('adam-eve-64-eves-pangs-and-the-birth-of-cain',63,2,'canon','genesis',3,16,3,$n$Genesis 3:16 — *in sorrow thou shalt bring forth children.* Eve's pangs are the sorrow in childbearing named in the sentence.$n$),
('adam-eve-64-eves-pangs-and-the-birth-of-cain',63,2,'canon','john',16,21,4,$n$John 16:21 — *A woman when she is in travail hath sorrow, because her hour is come.* The travail Eve cries from ends, as Yahusha (Jesus) says, in a child born.$n$),
('adam-eve-66-cain-slays-abel-and-seth-is-born',65,2,'canon','genesis',4,2,6,$n$Genesis 4:2 — *And Abel was a keeper of sheep, but Cain was a tiller of the ground.* The brothers who stayed together are named by the work that divided them.$n$),
('adam-eve-66-cain-slays-abel-and-seth-is-born',65,3,'canon','genesis',4,10,7,$n$Genesis 4:10 — *the voice of thy brother’s blood crieth unto me from the ground.* The blood Eve sees in a vision is the blood that cries from the ground.$n$),
('adam-eve-66-cain-slays-abel-and-seth-is-born',65,4,'canon','genesis',4,9,8,$n$Genesis 4:9 — *And he said, I know not: Am I my brother’s keeper?* Adam keeping the boys apart is the keeping that Cain refused.$n$),
('adam-eve-70-let-not-the-name-be-blotted-out',70,1,'canon','psalms',51,11,1,$n$Psalm 51:11 — *Cast me not away from thy presence.* Adam's prayer not to be cast out is David's prayer in the psalm.$n$),
('adam-eve-70-let-not-the-name-be-blotted-out',70,2,'canon','isaiah',66,22,2,$n$Isaiah 66:22 — *so shall your seed and your name remain.* The seed that is not cut off in this book is the seed that remains in Isaiah.$n$),
('adam-eve-71-the-true-light-and-the-living-life',71,2,'canon','psalms',36,9,1,$n$Psalm 36:9 — *For with thee is the fountain of life: in thy light shall we see light.* Adam's praise of the Light and the Living Life is the psalm's fountain and light.$n$),
('adam-eve-71-the-true-light-and-the-living-life',71,1,'canon','psalms',90,2,2,$n$Psalm 90:2 — *even from everlasting to everlasting, thou art Elohim (God).* Adam's eternal and supreme Elohim (God) is the everlasting Elohim (God) of the psalm.$n$),
('adam-eve-74-seth-would-go-to-the-gates-of-paradise',74,1,'canon','joshua',7,6,1,$n$Joshua 7:6 — *and put dust upon their heads.* Seth's dust on his head is the gesture of Joshua and the elders at the ark.$n$),
('adam-eve-74-seth-would-go-to-the-gates-of-paradise',74,2,'canon','psalms',38,3,2,$n$Psalm 38:3 — *There is no soundness in my flesh because of thine anger.* The pain Adam describes to Seth is the pain the psalmist ties to sin.$n$),
('adam-eve-75-the-prohibition-and-the-two-portions',75,1,'canon','genesis',2,17,1,$n$Genesis 2:17 — *in the day that thou eatest thereof thou shalt surely die.* Adam's prohibition is the commandment of Genesis with its penalty.$n$),
('adam-eve-75-the-prohibition-and-the-two-portions',75,1,'canon','genesis',2,9,2,$n$Genesis 2:9 — *the tree of life also in the midst of the garden, and the tree of knowledge of good and evil.* The tree in the midst of paradise is the tree Moses places next to the tree of life.$n$),
('adam-eve-77-seventy-blows-upon-the-body',77,1,'canon','deuteronomy',28,59,1,$n$Deuteronomy 28:59 — *the plagues of thy seed, even great plagues.* The blows laid on the body and on the race are the plagues on the seed in Moses.$n$),
('adam-eve-77-seventy-blows-upon-the-body',77,1,'canon','romans',5,12,2,$n$Romans 5:12 — *as by one man sin entered into the world, and death by sin.* What Adam says falls on all his race is what Paul says passed upon all men.$n$),
('adam-eve-9-half-of-thy-trouble',78,1,'canon','2-samuel',24,17,5,$n$2 Samuel 24:17 — *Lo, I have sinned, and I have done wickedly.* Eve's asking to take the pain is David's asking to take the stroke.$n$),
('adam-eve-9-half-of-thy-trouble',78,2,'canon','galatians',6,2,6,$n$Galatians 6:2 — *Bear ye one another’s burdens, and so fulfil the law of Messiah (Christ).* Eve asking for a part of Adam's pain is a burden borne for another.$n$),
('adam-eve-80-against-the-image-of-elohim',81,1,'canon','numbers',22,28,4,$n$Numbers 22:28 — *Yahuah (LORD) opened the mouth of the ass.* The speaking beast has its one plain precedent in Balaam's ass.$n$),
('adam-eve-80-against-the-image-of-elohim',81,1,'canon','genesis',9,2,5,$n$Genesis 9:2 — *And the fear of you and the dread of you shall be upon every beast of the earth.* The beasts' malice against Eve is the strain between man and beast in Genesis.$n$),
('adam-eve-79-oil-of-the-tree-of-mercy',83,1,'canon','james',5,14,4,$n$James 5:14 — *anointing him with oil in the name of Yahuah (Lord).* The oil sought for the sick Adam is the oil of anointing James names.$n$),
('adam-eve-79-oil-of-the-tree-of-mercy',83,1,'canon','psalms',103,3,5,$n$Psalm 103:3 — *Who forgiveth all thine iniquities; who healeth all thy diseases.* What the mourners beg for is what the psalm says Yahuah (the LORD) does.$n$),
('adam-eve-13-oil-of-mercy-in-the-end-of-the-times',84,1,'canon','revelation',22,2,5,$n$Revelation 22:2 — *the leaves of the tree were for the healing of the nations.* The tree of mercy withheld now is the tree of life at the end.$n$),
('adam-eve-13-oil-of-mercy-in-the-end-of-the-times',84,1,'canon','habakkuk',2,3,6,$n$Habakkuk 2:3 — *though it tarry, wait for it; because it will surely come.* The oil that waits for the end is the vision that waits for its time.$n$),
('adam-eve-86-six-days-and-herbs-of-fragrance',86,1,'canon','genesis',5,5,1,$n$Genesis 5:5 — *all the days that Adam lived were nine hundred and thirty years: and he died.* The fulfilled life Michael speaks of is the nine hundred and thirty years of Genesis.$n$),
('adam-eve-86-six-days-and-herbs-of-fragrance',86,2,'canon','song-of-solomon',4,14,2,$n$Song of Solomon 4:14 — *Spikenard and saffron; calamus and cinnamon.* The four herbs they carry home are four spices of the Song's garden.$n$),
('adam-eve-87-a-great-plague-for-all-our-generations',87,1,'canon','1-corinthians',15,22,1,$n$1 Corinthians 15:22 — *For as in Adam all die, even so in Messiah (Christ) shall all be made alive.* Adam's grief over the reach of the fall is answered in Paul by a reach of life.$n$),
('adam-eve-87-a-great-plague-for-all-our-generations',87,1,'canon','ezekiel',18,20,2,$n$Ezekiel 18:20 — *The son shall not bear the iniquity of the father.* The children's complaint against their parents is met by Ezekiel's word on whose sin is whose.$n$),
('adam-eve-90-blessed-art-thou-for-pity-on-thy-creature',90,1,'canon','psalms',145,9,1,$n$Psalm 145:9 — *his tender mercies are over all his works.* The blessing for pity on the creature rests on the mercy over all his works.$n$),
('adam-eve-90-blessed-art-thou-for-pity-on-thy-creature',90,1,'canon','luke',1,68,2,$n$Luke 1:68 — *for he hath visited and redeemed his people.* Adam's blessing for pity is Zechariah's blessing for a visit of redemption.$n$);
